00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:51.880
So quick recap,
because I know it's

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:54.940
been a long 20
minutes or whatever.

00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:57.910
When we last left our
heroes, algebraic codes,

00:00:57.910 --> 00:00:58.880
what was going on?

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:00.550
So we saw two problems.

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:03.130
We saw the unique decoding
problem and the list decoding

00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:03.650
problem.

00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:06.580
So the unique decoding
problem, our goal

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:09.130
was to handle a number
of errors which was less

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:12.135
than half the distance over--

00:01:12.135 --> 00:01:13.510
with less than
half the distance.

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:16.670
And for Reed-Solomon
codes, the distance--

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:20.000
sorry, that should be times N.
The distance is 1 minus R times

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:25.520
N about, and so we wanted
T to be about this times

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:26.690
N. Sorry for the typo.

00:01:26.690 --> 00:01:30.980
And if that's the case, then we
know when we see this received

00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:33.782
word that there's a unique
code word we can decode to

00:01:33.782 --> 00:01:35.240
and we want to do
that efficiently.

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:37.032
We also looked at the
list decoding problem

00:01:37.032 --> 00:01:39.260
where we were allowed to
take T a little bit bigger.

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:40.677
Again, there's an
N missing there.

00:01:40.677 --> 00:01:43.160
We want T to be 1
minus R times N. Sorry.

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:46.820
And the goal is to recover
all of the code words

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:50.427
in that ball and implicit in
that statement is that if we

00:01:50.427 --> 00:01:52.760
want to do that efficiently,
that there ought not be too

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:53.968
many code words in that ball.

00:01:53.968 --> 00:01:55.280
That's what we're hoping for.

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:56.930
And we saw an
algorithmic paradigm

00:01:56.930 --> 00:01:59.660
to do this, which
had three steps.

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:02.090
One, interpolate some
polynomial-- two,

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:05.720
assert that that polynomial
explains our target function F.

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:09.229
And three, use that
explanation to solve for F,

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:14.280
or solve for some small
positive list of possibilities.

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:14.780
OK.

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:16.310
So this is what
we did last time.

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:18.020
We saw this-- saw
several examples

00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:20.270
of this for both unique
decoding list decoding.

00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:23.330
And what we're going to do this
time for the next hour or so,

00:02:23.330 --> 00:02:26.240
is see how we can
improve on this.

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:27.380
There's still more to do.

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:29.853
Can we make these
algorithms faster?

00:02:29.853 --> 00:02:31.520
People were asking
about the complexity,

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:33.770
and I was, like,
yeah, polynomial time,

00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:35.062
do linear algebra, or whatever.

00:02:35.062 --> 00:02:37.353
We'll see how we can make
this faster near linear time.

00:02:37.353 --> 00:02:39.890
That's going to be our goal for
the second half of the talk.

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:42.440
Can we get smaller
list sizes, and can we

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:43.712
work over smaller fields?

00:02:43.712 --> 00:02:45.170
So last time we
were always working

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:46.880
over these big, big fields.

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:49.748
But like often, we really
want to be working over F2.

00:02:49.748 --> 00:02:50.540
How can we do that?

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:52.380
So today we're going
to-- or in this talk,

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:53.963
we're going to answer
those questions.

00:02:53.963 --> 00:02:56.150
So techniques to
improve this paradigm.

00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:58.670
So here's going to be
a list of techniques.

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:02.910
And along the way, we'll
see where they stop working

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:04.703
and what the open questions are.

00:03:04.703 --> 00:03:06.620
So the first technique
I'm going to talk about

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:09.410
is locality, or
leveraging locality.

00:03:09.410 --> 00:03:10.850
So how can we
speed up algorithms

00:03:10.850 --> 00:03:13.070
by running them on smaller bits?

00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:14.790
This is going to be
real fast because we

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:16.530
have a whole two
hours of locality

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:19.920
later, or more, by Elena.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:22.167
So this will just be a teaser.

00:03:22.167 --> 00:03:24.000
The second technique
I'm going to talk about

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:25.830
is distance amplification.

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:28.530
So basically, one of the punch
lines of technique one is

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:29.370
it's--

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:31.380
we can make faster
algorithms, but at the cost

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:32.688
of getting worse parameters.

00:03:32.688 --> 00:03:34.230
And so then we'll
see some techniques

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:37.320
for getting the parameters
back in line after we do that.

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:39.120
The third technique
I'll want to talk about

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:40.037
is alphabet reduction.

00:03:40.037 --> 00:03:42.240
We'll see two different
ways to do this.

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:46.020
One, code concatenation,
and the second I'll

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:48.870
just mention very briefly
using algebraic geometry codes.

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:51.930
And if time, I have
a fourth technique

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:54.510
to talk about, which is
how to exploit structure

00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:56.160
in the codes and the algorithms.

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:57.810
But I'm not sure if
we'll get to that.

00:03:57.810 --> 00:03:59.090
But if we do, then it's there.

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:00.600
And if we don't,
then that's fine.

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:02.850
So we'll talk about all of
these different techniques.

00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:06.120
And, yeah, along the way
highlight several open problems

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:08.640
and what's still
left to do here.

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:10.950
And as usual, as with
last time, please

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:14.040
do interrupt me
and ask questions.

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:17.029
So I anticipate I won't be
able to get to technique four,

00:04:17.029 --> 00:04:19.700
even if I don't get to some of
these techniques, that's fine.

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:21.810
I want to make sure
people understand.

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:22.310
Cool.

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:22.810
OK.

00:04:22.810 --> 00:04:24.530
So let's get started
with locality.

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:26.780
And as I mentioned before,
we have a whole talk series

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:29.360
on locality later in
the workshop by Elena.

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:31.460
Where's Elena?

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:32.420
Elena, yay.

00:04:32.420 --> 00:04:35.330
So this is just a teaser.

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:37.160
So the idea of
locality, at least

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:40.220
in the context of algorithms
for algebraic codes

00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:44.090
is, we want to somehow
either make a big code out

00:04:44.090 --> 00:04:46.730
of little codes, or view a code
that we already have as being

00:04:46.730 --> 00:04:47.872
made out of littler codes.

00:04:47.872 --> 00:04:49.580
So let me see, I'm
just going to describe

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:50.810
this with a couple
of examples, rather

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:53.510
than a formal definition since
this is a hand-wavy thing.

00:04:53.510 --> 00:04:55.760
So one example of
this is tensor codes.

00:04:55.760 --> 00:04:57.350
So suppose we
start with the code

00:04:57.350 --> 00:05:02.618
C, subset of sigma to the
N. We can define C tensor C.

00:05:02.618 --> 00:05:04.910
So this is going to be a code
with longer block length.

00:05:04.910 --> 00:05:07.250
It'll have block length
N squared instead of N

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:08.370
over the same alphabet.

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:09.870
We're going to
define it as follows.

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:11.450
So it's going to
be the set of all N

00:05:11.450 --> 00:05:15.990
by N matrices A, such that all
of the rows of A lie in my base

00:05:15.990 --> 00:05:18.580
code, and all of the columns
of A lie in my base code.

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:20.040
So if I look at
any row or column,

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:22.590
I get something
in the base code.

00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:24.120
And more generally,
you can define

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:26.710
C tensor C tensor dot to dot
tensor C in the natural way.

00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:29.357
So you take an M dimensional
tensor, and every--

00:05:29.357 --> 00:05:31.440
this one and that one, and
that one, and whatever,

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:34.690
need to live in the code.

00:05:34.690 --> 00:05:36.380
This make sense?

00:05:36.380 --> 00:05:40.040
And this is some way of
making a big code where

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:43.910
it has lots of little codes in
it, because I have this code

00:05:43.910 --> 00:05:44.930
word in my big code.

00:05:44.930 --> 00:05:46.940
And then there's all
these little code

00:05:46.940 --> 00:05:50.120
words from the little
code floating around.

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:53.888
Another place where we see
this is in multivariate codes.

00:05:53.888 --> 00:05:55.430
For example, I'll
just draw a picture

00:05:55.430 --> 00:05:57.470
of bivariate Reed-Muller codes.

00:05:57.470 --> 00:05:59.450
So a bivariate
Reed-Muller code, this

00:05:59.450 --> 00:06:02.000
is like the bivariate analog
of the Reed-Solomon code

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:03.300
that we saw last time.

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:05.240
So the code words
are, again, I'm

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:09.800
going to think of them as
matrices where the rows are

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:11.930
indexed by field elements beta.

00:06:11.930 --> 00:06:14.660
The columns are indexed
by field melons alpha.

00:06:14.660 --> 00:06:17.480
And what goes in
the alpha beta place

00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:20.930
is F of alpha beta for some
low degree polynomial F.

00:06:20.930 --> 00:06:24.260
So this is the Reed-Muller code,
the bivariate Reed-Muller code

00:06:24.260 --> 00:06:27.110
of dimension, or of
degree at most K--

00:06:27.110 --> 00:06:30.250
or of degree K minus 1.

00:06:30.250 --> 00:06:33.940
And this has this same
notion of locality

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:37.000
in that we can find lots
of little Reed-Solomon code

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:38.530
words floating around here.

00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:42.330
So if I take any
line in S squared,

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:47.140
so I have this space F squared,
and I look at any line here.

00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:50.140
And I look at the
restriction of this code word

00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:51.910
to that line, what
I'm going to get

00:06:51.910 --> 00:06:54.307
is a univariate
polynomial, right?

00:06:54.307 --> 00:06:56.140
So basically, just a
Reed-Solomon code word.

00:06:56.140 --> 00:07:01.060
So any line that I take in
this square, the picture

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:02.560
isn't quite right
because it doesn't

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:03.250
look like a field structure.

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:05.167
But you imagine it wraps
around, or something.

00:07:05.167 --> 00:07:07.720
And any line that I
take in this square

00:07:07.720 --> 00:07:09.730
looks like a
Reed-Solomon code word.

00:07:09.730 --> 00:07:11.290
So I have all these
little code words

00:07:11.290 --> 00:07:14.710
from some other code
embedded in my big code.

00:07:14.710 --> 00:07:17.080
Another example is lifted codes.

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:20.260
So I could imagine
instead of this definition

00:07:20.260 --> 00:07:22.875
here where I say, OK, I'm
going to define it like this.

00:07:22.875 --> 00:07:25.000
And, oh, hey, it just
happens to have this property

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:26.500
that when I restrict
to a line I get

00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:28.012
something in my original code.

00:07:28.012 --> 00:07:30.470
I can actually just define the
code that way to begin with.

00:07:30.470 --> 00:07:33.110
I could define it
to be evaluations

00:07:33.110 --> 00:07:34.880
of all of the
bivariate polynomials

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:37.010
F, all of my
bivariate functions F

00:07:37.010 --> 00:07:39.320
so that when I
restrict to a line,

00:07:39.320 --> 00:07:42.378
I get something in
the code, right.

00:07:42.378 --> 00:07:43.670
This turns out to be different.

00:07:43.670 --> 00:07:46.370
This is called a lifted code.

00:07:46.370 --> 00:07:51.200
And you might think that if C is
a Reed-Solomon code, that these

00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:53.810
are actually the same
that the Reed-Muller code

00:07:53.810 --> 00:07:57.230
words are all the things
that restrict to lines.

00:07:57.230 --> 00:07:58.860
In some parameter
regimes, that's true.

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:00.805
It's actually not true,
in general, in fact.

00:08:00.805 --> 00:08:02.210
And I think, Elena,
are you going

00:08:02.210 --> 00:08:04.407
to talk about that,
lifting codes?

00:08:04.407 --> 00:08:05.240
Maybe, a little bit?

00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:05.740
OK.

00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:08.083
So stick around for Elena's
talk to see more about that,

00:08:08.083 --> 00:08:08.750
or ask me later.

00:08:08.750 --> 00:08:11.700
This is a cool phenomenon that
is not related to this talk.

00:08:11.700 --> 00:08:13.730
So I'm going to skip by
it, but it's a fun fact.

00:08:13.730 --> 00:08:18.590
Anyway, so these are
three different examples

00:08:18.590 --> 00:08:21.380
of ways that natural codes
we can come up with that

00:08:21.380 --> 00:08:24.800
have little codes embedded
in some bigger code.

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:29.030
And the point for algorithms is
that if we have a big code made

00:08:29.030 --> 00:08:33.280
out of little codes,
well, this gives us

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:34.480
some algorithmic leverage.

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:38.094
Because then we can run our
decoder on these little code

00:08:38.094 --> 00:08:41.860
words and patch them
together somehow.

00:08:41.860 --> 00:08:43.809
And the hope is that,
well, because the code

00:08:43.809 --> 00:08:46.210
words are little,
then this is going

00:08:46.210 --> 00:08:47.650
to run very quickly, right?

00:08:47.650 --> 00:08:50.380
Even if I have an
algorithm for my base code

00:08:50.380 --> 00:08:55.120
that takes time, like,
N squared, or something,

00:08:55.120 --> 00:08:59.260
if the whole block length is N
squared, then all of a sudden

00:08:59.260 --> 00:09:02.950
that's linear time, not
quadratic time, like, yay.

00:09:02.950 --> 00:09:04.648
So that's the hope.

00:09:04.648 --> 00:09:06.940
Of course, there are lots of
subtleties with this hope,

00:09:06.940 --> 00:09:10.240
like, OK, how do we
patch things together,

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:13.010
and which little code words
do we choose to run on?

00:09:13.010 --> 00:09:14.510
There are tons of
little code words.

00:09:14.510 --> 00:09:17.740
If we do it on all of them,
that's going to be too slow,

00:09:17.740 --> 00:09:19.653
and lots of things like that.

00:09:19.653 --> 00:09:21.070
And then the thing
I actually want

00:09:21.070 --> 00:09:22.990
to talk about here is
that the parameters are

00:09:22.990 --> 00:09:24.280
going to get worse.

00:09:24.280 --> 00:09:27.370
So these first two things
I'll just sweep under the rug

00:09:27.370 --> 00:09:29.560
and say, for all of
these different examples,

00:09:29.560 --> 00:09:31.450
there are ways to deal with it.

00:09:31.450 --> 00:09:35.830
But I want to just mention
what happens to the parameters.

00:09:35.830 --> 00:09:39.980
So, for example,
for tensor codes,

00:09:39.980 --> 00:09:42.920
actually, I feel like I've
just been barreling on.

00:09:42.920 --> 00:09:45.900
Are there questions?

00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:47.490
I haven't said
anything super precise,

00:09:47.490 --> 00:09:51.500
but basic paradigm make sense?

00:09:51.500 --> 00:09:52.010
Cool.

00:09:52.010 --> 00:09:52.340
OK.

00:09:52.340 --> 00:09:52.910
So, yeah.

00:09:52.910 --> 00:09:54.560
So we need to figure out how
to patch things together.

00:09:54.560 --> 00:09:56.852
You need to figure out which
of these little code words

00:09:56.852 --> 00:09:58.460
you want to leverage.

00:09:58.460 --> 00:10:00.530
How you do that is a
case-by-case basis.

00:10:00.530 --> 00:10:02.477
But inevitably, the
parameters are going

00:10:02.477 --> 00:10:03.560
to get a little bit worse.

00:10:03.560 --> 00:10:05.540
So for example,
for tensor codes,

00:10:05.540 --> 00:10:07.340
let's say that
your base code C--

00:10:07.340 --> 00:10:11.150
say it has really high rate,
and reasonably small distance.

00:10:11.150 --> 00:10:14.390
So let's say rate 1 minus
epsilon and distance epsilon.

00:10:14.390 --> 00:10:17.120
So we saw last time
this-- this is optimal.

00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:19.130
This meets the Singleton
bound, like, hurrah, we

00:10:19.130 --> 00:10:21.540
have this optimal base code C.

00:10:21.540 --> 00:10:24.480
Let's consider C
tensor C. It turns out

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:26.820
that to get the
parameters for T tensor C,

00:10:26.820 --> 00:10:29.770
you basically just square the
distance and square the rate.

00:10:29.770 --> 00:10:33.420
So squaring the rate,
that's still a high rate

00:10:33.420 --> 00:10:38.340
code, like, 1 minus epsilon
squared is to first order

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:40.500
1 minus 2 epsilon
is still close to 1.

00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:42.000
But the distance is now--

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:44.760
or the relative distance
is now epsilon squared.

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:46.620
But we wanted it to
be like 2 epsilon.

00:10:46.620 --> 00:10:50.040
So that's not good.

00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:52.230
So that this is not optimal.

00:10:52.230 --> 00:10:55.290
So whenever we do
this, we're moving away

00:10:55.290 --> 00:10:58.260
from this optimal
parameter we want to be in.

00:10:58.260 --> 00:11:00.680
And so that's going to
motivate the next technique

00:11:00.680 --> 00:11:01.680
that we're going to use.

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:04.110
I'm not going to talk
anymore about locality

00:11:04.110 --> 00:11:06.600
because that's, again, just
a teaser for Elena's talk.

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:08.225
But the next thing
I want to talk about

00:11:08.225 --> 00:11:09.425
is distance amplification.

00:11:09.425 --> 00:11:11.550
I will go into a little
bit more detail about this.

00:11:11.550 --> 00:11:13.710
And the idea here is,
OK, let's say we've

00:11:13.710 --> 00:11:15.870
done this local thing somehow.

00:11:15.870 --> 00:11:18.210
But now, we have a code
that has a fast algorithm

00:11:18.210 --> 00:11:19.920
but bad parameters.

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:22.060
How do we get the
parameters back in line?

00:11:22.060 --> 00:11:25.810
And distance amplification
is a technique to do that.

00:11:25.810 --> 00:11:27.220
So, yeah.

00:11:27.220 --> 00:11:29.620
So the basic idea is
we'd like to transform

00:11:29.620 --> 00:11:32.890
a code we have into one
with better parameters,

00:11:32.890 --> 00:11:36.550
ideally, in a way
that respects--

00:11:36.550 --> 00:11:38.350
that plays nice with
algorithms that we

00:11:38.350 --> 00:11:40.750
might have for that code.

00:11:40.750 --> 00:11:42.550
And so today what I'm
going to talk about

00:11:42.550 --> 00:11:45.790
is a particular technique known
as AEL distance amplification.

00:11:45.790 --> 00:11:48.490
So AEL here stands for
Alon, Edmonds, and Luby

00:11:48.490 --> 00:11:51.370
after this paper from 1995.

00:11:51.370 --> 00:11:54.640
And this technique has been
used frequently since then

00:11:54.640 --> 00:11:59.470
in the algorithms for
algebraic codes literature.

00:11:59.470 --> 00:12:03.010
I'll mention just one paper,
this paper by Kopparty, Meir,

00:12:03.010 --> 00:12:05.758
Ron-Zewi, and Saraf
from 2015 just because I

00:12:05.758 --> 00:12:08.050
think this is the first paper
where I really appreciate

00:12:08.050 --> 00:12:09.217
the power of this technique.

00:12:09.217 --> 00:12:10.600
But it's been used--

00:12:10.600 --> 00:12:13.635
it's in this algorithmic context
in a lot of other places.

00:12:13.635 --> 00:12:15.010
I should mention,
also, there are

00:12:15.010 --> 00:12:18.070
lots of other versions of
distance amplification.

00:12:18.070 --> 00:12:21.490
So, for example, you might
be familiar with this sort

00:12:21.490 --> 00:12:23.785
of classic ABNNR.

00:12:23.785 --> 00:12:26.800
Distance amplification is also
a more recent version also

00:12:26.800 --> 00:12:28.820
with range amplification
by Cohen and Yankovitz,

00:12:28.820 --> 00:12:30.190
and many others.

00:12:30.190 --> 00:12:32.200
But today just to
focus on one thing,

00:12:32.200 --> 00:12:35.930
I'm going to look
at AEL distance.

00:12:35.930 --> 00:12:38.240
So here's how this works.

00:12:38.240 --> 00:12:41.480
So we're going to have two
ingredients that we start with.

00:12:41.480 --> 00:12:43.010
The first is our base code.

00:12:43.010 --> 00:12:47.180
So we need a high
rate code C, and we'll

00:12:47.180 --> 00:12:51.020
think of C as having a
fast decoding algorithm.

00:12:51.020 --> 00:12:53.292
But maybe it can't
handle very much error.

00:12:53.292 --> 00:12:55.250
So imagine this is
something we get after doing

00:12:55.250 --> 00:12:56.280
this locality thing.

00:12:56.280 --> 00:12:58.193
So we have a code C,
it's very high rate,

00:12:58.193 --> 00:13:00.860
like, maybe we've tensored it up
so it still has very high rate,

00:13:00.860 --> 00:13:02.570
but it has teeny tiny distance.

00:13:02.570 --> 00:13:04.130
We have super fast
algorithms for it,

00:13:04.130 --> 00:13:07.100
but it can only handle a little
bit of error corresponding

00:13:07.100 --> 00:13:08.480
to that teeny tiny distance.

00:13:08.480 --> 00:13:12.877
And we want to make
it handle more error.

00:13:12.877 --> 00:13:14.210
So that's ingredient number one.

00:13:14.210 --> 00:13:17.120
Ingredient number two is a
balanced bipartite expander

00:13:17.120 --> 00:13:17.990
graph.

00:13:17.990 --> 00:13:20.130
So it'll look like this.

00:13:20.130 --> 00:13:21.110
So it's-- what is this?

00:13:21.110 --> 00:13:24.920
It's a bipartite
graph, and it has

00:13:24.920 --> 00:13:29.240
the following nice
pseudorandomness property

00:13:29.240 --> 00:13:32.870
where if I look at any
set S on the right,

00:13:32.870 --> 00:13:35.590
small enough set S on
the right, like this one.

00:13:35.590 --> 00:13:37.810
And I look at the
vertices on the left

00:13:37.810 --> 00:13:42.310
and I ask, how much do these
vertices touch that set?

00:13:42.310 --> 00:13:46.150
Most vertices on the left should
touch that set about as much

00:13:46.150 --> 00:13:48.080
as you would expect.

00:13:48.080 --> 00:13:51.610
So for example
here, if I highlight

00:13:51.610 --> 00:13:53.950
all of the edges coming
out of this set, OK,

00:13:53.950 --> 00:13:56.900
this set has size M
over 3 in this example.

00:13:56.900 --> 00:14:00.820
So I should expect a
third of everyone's--

00:14:00.820 --> 00:14:03.790
like a third of this vertices
edges to touch the set.

00:14:03.790 --> 00:14:05.582
And indeed, that's the
case for this vertex

00:14:05.582 --> 00:14:07.248
that has three edges
coming out, and one

00:14:07.248 --> 00:14:08.290
of them touches the set.

00:14:08.290 --> 00:14:10.990
And that's true for
most of these vertices.

00:14:10.990 --> 00:14:12.460
But it's not true for this one.

00:14:12.460 --> 00:14:14.122
This one has two.

00:14:14.122 --> 00:14:16.330
So it's OK if there's a few
that have too many edges.

00:14:16.330 --> 00:14:18.490
But most of them should
have about what you expect.

00:14:18.490 --> 00:14:21.060
I'm not going to be any
more precise about this.

00:14:21.060 --> 00:14:23.470
Like I said, the first talk
was the one where I actually

00:14:23.470 --> 00:14:24.512
did a little bit of math.

00:14:24.512 --> 00:14:26.740
This talk, very
hand-wavy high level.

00:14:26.740 --> 00:14:28.150
You can ask me
for details later.

00:14:28.150 --> 00:14:28.390
OK.

00:14:28.390 --> 00:14:30.700
So this is what I mean by
balanced bipartite expander

00:14:30.700 --> 00:14:31.970
graph.

00:14:31.970 --> 00:14:34.808
So what is this distance
amplification technique?

00:14:34.808 --> 00:14:36.350
So say we have these
two ingredients.

00:14:36.350 --> 00:14:38.840
We have our base code C, and
we have our expander graph.

00:14:38.840 --> 00:14:40.250
What are we going to do?

00:14:40.250 --> 00:14:42.250
So I'm going to start
with the code word from C,

00:14:42.250 --> 00:14:43.833
and what I'm going
to do is, I'm going

00:14:43.833 --> 00:14:46.100
to show you how to
transform that into a code

00:14:46.100 --> 00:14:48.380
word from some
new code that I'll

00:14:48.380 --> 00:14:50.895
define by this transformation.

00:14:50.895 --> 00:14:52.520
So the first thing
I'm going to do to C

00:14:52.520 --> 00:14:53.780
is, I'm going to chop it up.

00:14:53.780 --> 00:14:58.100
So I'm going to chop it up into
chunks of some particular size.

00:14:58.100 --> 00:15:00.620
And then I'm going to take
each one of those chunks

00:15:00.620 --> 00:15:04.880
and encode them with a little
tiny Reed-Solomon code,

00:15:04.880 --> 00:15:07.760
or maybe a folded Reed-Solomon
code, or a code of my choice

00:15:07.760 --> 00:15:09.850
that has the
properties that I want.

00:15:09.850 --> 00:15:11.630
And basically, the
idea is I'm going

00:15:11.630 --> 00:15:14.960
to end up with a
final code that mimics

00:15:14.960 --> 00:15:17.960
the properties of this little
code, but like in a big scale

00:15:17.960 --> 00:15:20.790
and with linear time algorithms.

00:15:20.790 --> 00:15:23.540
So I'll encode each chunk with
a little Reed-Solomon code.

00:15:23.540 --> 00:15:26.440
And now, it is expander time.

00:15:26.440 --> 00:15:28.130
Yay.

00:15:28.130 --> 00:15:29.964
There's less enthusiasm.

00:15:32.870 --> 00:15:34.550
Now, it's expander time.

00:15:34.550 --> 00:15:35.112
Yay.

00:15:35.112 --> 00:15:36.320
All right, there we go, good.

00:15:36.320 --> 00:15:37.350
So now it's expander time.

00:15:37.350 --> 00:15:38.725
And so what we're
going to do is,

00:15:38.725 --> 00:15:40.520
we're going to take
our bipartite expander

00:15:40.520 --> 00:15:44.300
and we're going to
use it to shuffle up

00:15:44.300 --> 00:15:47.132
the coordinates here, like,
shuffle up the symbols.

00:15:47.132 --> 00:15:48.590
So what is that
going to look like?

00:15:48.590 --> 00:15:52.593
I'm going to instantiate
a new code word over here.

00:15:52.593 --> 00:15:54.260
This is going to be
our final code word.

00:15:54.260 --> 00:15:57.995
And I'm going to populate it
with the symbols from this code

00:15:57.995 --> 00:16:00.390
word, but sort of munged up
according to the expander.

00:16:00.390 --> 00:16:02.360
So what does that look like?

00:16:02.360 --> 00:16:05.180
So let's suppose that this
first symbol here-- oh,

00:16:05.180 --> 00:16:07.370
and I should say the
vertices of the expander

00:16:07.370 --> 00:16:12.290
correspond to chunks
in this vector here.

00:16:12.290 --> 00:16:16.820
So this first chunk, let's say
it has three symbols in it,

00:16:16.820 --> 00:16:18.530
it corresponds to this vertex.

00:16:18.530 --> 00:16:20.430
And the number of
symbols in this chunk

00:16:20.430 --> 00:16:22.800
should be the same as the
degree of the vertex, which

00:16:22.800 --> 00:16:24.545
fortunately, it is
in my little example.

00:16:24.545 --> 00:16:25.920
And now, what I'm
going to do is,

00:16:25.920 --> 00:16:28.740
I'm basically just going
to shift these symbols

00:16:28.740 --> 00:16:30.130
across the expander.

00:16:30.130 --> 00:16:32.130
So this alpha, that's
the first symbol,

00:16:32.130 --> 00:16:34.957
so I'll send it across
the first edge over there.

00:16:34.957 --> 00:16:36.540
The beta is the
second symbol, so I'll

00:16:36.540 --> 00:16:38.197
send it across the second edge.

00:16:38.197 --> 00:16:39.780
And gamma is the
third symbol, so I'll

00:16:39.780 --> 00:16:42.720
send it across the
third edge like that.

00:16:42.720 --> 00:16:44.310
Does that make sense?

00:16:44.310 --> 00:16:46.230
And then I'm going
to do this for all

00:16:46.230 --> 00:16:47.303
of the different symbols.

00:16:47.303 --> 00:16:49.470
I'm not going to do that
many PowerPoint animations,

00:16:49.470 --> 00:16:52.020
but you can see
where that's going.

00:16:52.020 --> 00:16:54.780
So, for example, down here if
I had alpha prime, beta prime,

00:16:54.780 --> 00:16:58.470
gamma prime, those would
end up in the symbols that

00:16:58.470 --> 00:17:02.850
were connected to this vertex.

00:17:02.850 --> 00:17:04.849
Does this process make sense?

00:17:04.849 --> 00:17:07.250
It's very obnoxious
to write down formally

00:17:07.250 --> 00:17:10.390
but, hopefully, a picture is OK.

00:17:10.390 --> 00:17:10.890
OK.

00:17:10.890 --> 00:17:13.950
And so then once we do
this, this green thing here

00:17:13.950 --> 00:17:16.230
on the right, this is going
to be our new code word.

00:17:16.230 --> 00:17:20.369
And here, just like with
folded Reed-Solomon codes

00:17:20.369 --> 00:17:22.079
that we saw last
time, we're going

00:17:22.079 --> 00:17:23.535
to take our alphabet
to be larger

00:17:23.535 --> 00:17:24.660
than our original alphabet.

00:17:24.660 --> 00:17:28.109
So our alphabet symbols are
now these folded symbols.

00:17:28.109 --> 00:17:30.510
They're symbols--
they're vector-valued.

00:17:30.510 --> 00:17:33.180
So our alphabet is
going to be F to the S

00:17:33.180 --> 00:17:37.680
where S is whatever the length
of these little chunks are.

00:17:37.680 --> 00:17:40.070
Does that make sense?

00:17:40.070 --> 00:17:40.570
OK.

00:17:40.570 --> 00:17:44.290
So now, I have a code
which I define by--

00:17:44.290 --> 00:17:47.620
for every code word
in C, do this process,

00:17:47.620 --> 00:17:48.970
get this code word.

00:17:48.970 --> 00:17:52.510
And my new code is all
of these new code words.

00:17:52.510 --> 00:17:54.700
Why have we done this?

00:17:54.700 --> 00:17:57.070
Let's see what
happens when we want

00:17:57.070 --> 00:17:58.600
to say correct some errors.

00:17:58.600 --> 00:18:01.570
And hopefully, I'll describe
the decoding algorithm

00:18:01.570 --> 00:18:03.050
and that will
explain why we wanted

00:18:03.050 --> 00:18:04.300
to do this in the first place.

00:18:04.300 --> 00:18:06.830
And then we'll kind of wave
my hands about analyzing it.

00:18:06.830 --> 00:18:07.330
OK.

00:18:07.330 --> 00:18:09.100
So suppose that there
are some errors.

00:18:09.100 --> 00:18:12.070
And remember, my symbols here
are these big mega symbols.

00:18:12.070 --> 00:18:13.570
That means errors
are going to occur

00:18:13.570 --> 00:18:15.710
at the level of mega
symbols like that.

00:18:15.710 --> 00:18:16.210
OK.

00:18:16.210 --> 00:18:17.770
There are some errors like that.

00:18:17.770 --> 00:18:20.382
And let's see what the
decoding algorithm does.

00:18:20.382 --> 00:18:22.840
So the first thing we're going
to do, at least as a thought

00:18:22.840 --> 00:18:23.800
experiment, is--

00:18:23.800 --> 00:18:25.870
let's imagine
propagating these errors

00:18:25.870 --> 00:18:27.430
backwards to where
they came from.

00:18:27.430 --> 00:18:30.490
So we're going to propagate them
back across the expander graph.

00:18:30.490 --> 00:18:34.000
And so there were
six little symbols

00:18:34.000 --> 00:18:35.500
in error here--
two mega symbols.

00:18:35.500 --> 00:18:37.960
And we have these six little
symbols in error here.

00:18:37.960 --> 00:18:41.800
But now, they're all spread
out over all the mega symbols.

00:18:41.800 --> 00:18:44.860
And the property of
this bipartite expander

00:18:44.860 --> 00:18:50.380
was that for any set that
looks like this, most

00:18:50.380 --> 00:18:53.320
of the vertices over here
touch it in about the number

00:18:53.320 --> 00:18:54.380
you'd expect.

00:18:54.380 --> 00:18:57.550
And so what that means is that
most of the chunks over here

00:18:57.550 --> 00:19:00.190
have about the expected
number of errors.

00:19:00.190 --> 00:19:02.110
So what we've
basically done here

00:19:02.110 --> 00:19:04.210
with the pseudorandomness
of this expander graph

00:19:04.210 --> 00:19:08.890
is turned worst case errors into
something like random errors.

00:19:08.890 --> 00:19:10.480
Each chunk has about
what we'd expect,

00:19:10.480 --> 00:19:14.090
or most chunks have
about what we'd expect.

00:19:14.090 --> 00:19:15.910
And that's great,
because if we, let's say,

00:19:15.910 --> 00:19:18.040
design this little
Reed-Solomon code here

00:19:18.040 --> 00:19:21.070
to handle the amount of
errors that we expect,

00:19:21.070 --> 00:19:23.770
that that means that most
chunks we can actually correctly

00:19:23.770 --> 00:19:24.430
decode.

00:19:24.430 --> 00:19:28.690
So let's try to decode those
little Reed-Solomon codes.

00:19:28.690 --> 00:19:31.512
And almost all of them
will turn out well.

00:19:31.512 --> 00:19:33.220
Of course, there might
be a few vertices,

00:19:33.220 --> 00:19:35.350
like this one, that touch
the bad set too much

00:19:35.350 --> 00:19:37.225
and they have too many
errors, and that one's

00:19:37.225 --> 00:19:38.950
going to be wrong.

00:19:38.950 --> 00:19:41.750
But it's not too many of them.

00:19:41.750 --> 00:19:43.160
So now, we're back here.

00:19:43.160 --> 00:19:46.340
We have that most of the symbols
are correct except for this one

00:19:46.340 --> 00:19:47.000
maybe.

00:19:47.000 --> 00:19:50.270
So our next step is we're
going to just unchop things up.

00:19:50.270 --> 00:19:52.670
So again, we get
most symbols that

00:19:52.670 --> 00:19:54.950
are correct except for
a few that are wrong.

00:19:54.950 --> 00:19:58.400
But now, we can use the
properties of C, right?

00:19:58.400 --> 00:20:00.650
Remember C, capital
C, was a code

00:20:00.650 --> 00:20:03.860
that had a really fast
decoding algorithm,

00:20:03.860 --> 00:20:05.840
but it couldn't handle
very many errors.

00:20:05.840 --> 00:20:08.360
But now, look, we
have way fewer errors

00:20:08.360 --> 00:20:11.660
now than we had to begin with
so we can run C's algorithm.

00:20:11.660 --> 00:20:12.560
And that works.

00:20:12.560 --> 00:20:15.180
And then we can correct
the whole thing.

00:20:15.180 --> 00:20:17.300
So that's the basic idea.

00:20:17.300 --> 00:20:20.120
Does this make sense?

00:20:20.120 --> 00:20:21.353
Who has questions?

00:20:24.420 --> 00:20:24.920
OK.

00:20:24.920 --> 00:20:28.940
So let me wave my hands
about parameters real quick.

00:20:28.940 --> 00:20:31.580
What is the rate of this
code and how many errors

00:20:31.580 --> 00:20:34.020
can it handle, let's say, if
we wanted a unique decoding?

00:20:34.020 --> 00:20:34.520
OK.

00:20:34.520 --> 00:20:36.995
Well, C had high rate.

00:20:36.995 --> 00:20:39.620
This step of chopping things up
doesn't change the rate at all,

00:20:39.620 --> 00:20:41.150
it just repackages stuff.

00:20:41.150 --> 00:20:43.220
This step does change the rate.

00:20:43.220 --> 00:20:48.533
And, basically,
the rate of this,

00:20:48.533 --> 00:20:50.450
the amount that the rate
blows up in this step

00:20:50.450 --> 00:20:52.430
is, basically,
just by multiplying

00:20:52.430 --> 00:20:55.713
by the rate of whatever the
inner Reed-Solomon code was.

00:20:55.713 --> 00:20:57.380
This step doesn't
change the rate at all

00:20:57.380 --> 00:20:59.338
because, again, it's just
scrambling things up.

00:20:59.338 --> 00:21:01.550
So the rate of the
whole thing is the rate

00:21:01.550 --> 00:21:03.290
of the original
code times the rate

00:21:03.290 --> 00:21:04.850
of whatever this little
Reed-Solomon code is.

00:21:04.850 --> 00:21:06.808
If the rate of the original
code is close to 1,

00:21:06.808 --> 00:21:08.183
then the rate of
this whole thing

00:21:08.183 --> 00:21:10.808
is close to the rate of whatever
the original Reed-Solomon code

00:21:10.808 --> 00:21:11.390
was.

00:21:11.390 --> 00:21:13.160
And how many errors
can it correct?

00:21:13.160 --> 00:21:15.020
Well, the error
correction was really

00:21:15.020 --> 00:21:17.660
based on what could the
little inner Reed-Solomon code

00:21:17.660 --> 00:21:20.360
do with the expected
number of errors.

00:21:20.360 --> 00:21:23.090
So it can correct, basically,
the fraction of errors

00:21:23.090 --> 00:21:25.050
that the original
Reed-Solomon code could.

00:21:25.050 --> 00:21:25.850
So that's great.

00:21:25.850 --> 00:21:28.370
We've inherited this
optimal trade-off

00:21:28.370 --> 00:21:30.680
between rate and distance
from the Reed-Solomon code.

00:21:30.680 --> 00:21:34.040
But now, we actually
have a much longer code.

00:21:34.040 --> 00:21:36.170
How long does all
this algorithm take?

00:21:36.170 --> 00:21:38.080
What's the complexity
of this algorithm?

00:21:38.080 --> 00:21:38.580
OK.

00:21:38.580 --> 00:21:43.020
Munging up symbols can be done
in, basically, linear time.

00:21:43.020 --> 00:21:47.560
Decoding, right, maybe
this is slow relative

00:21:47.560 --> 00:21:48.810
to these little block lengths.

00:21:48.810 --> 00:21:49.935
But these are really short.

00:21:49.935 --> 00:21:53.520
So overall, this is also really
fast, basically, linear time.

00:21:53.520 --> 00:21:55.775
And then unchopping
and correcting,

00:21:55.775 --> 00:21:57.150
right, the whole
point was that C

00:21:57.150 --> 00:21:59.830
had this really fast, maybe near
linear time decoding algorithm.

00:21:59.830 --> 00:22:01.830
So altogether, this
whole decoding process

00:22:01.830 --> 00:22:04.620
can be near linear
time, but we've

00:22:04.620 --> 00:22:07.890
inherited those optimal
combinatorial properties

00:22:07.890 --> 00:22:10.020
from the inner
Reed-Solomon tree.

00:22:10.020 --> 00:22:11.560
Does that make sense?

00:22:11.560 --> 00:22:13.291
Cool.

00:22:13.291 --> 00:22:16.690
STUDENT: If we use the
folded Reed-Solomon code,

00:22:16.690 --> 00:22:17.530
what changes?

00:22:17.530 --> 00:22:18.520
PROFESSOR: Great, yeah.

00:22:18.520 --> 00:22:20.770
So what changes if we use
a folded Reed-Solomon code?

00:22:20.770 --> 00:22:23.230
So for unique
decoding, not too much,

00:22:23.230 --> 00:22:25.630
but this is a perfect
segue into my next slide.

00:22:25.630 --> 00:22:28.540
What if we want to do
list decoding, or-- yeah.

00:22:28.540 --> 00:22:30.550
STUDENT: A question about the--

00:22:30.550 --> 00:22:32.770
in the last column,
you say, OK, I'm

00:22:32.770 --> 00:22:36.400
going to attack this
block of three letters

00:22:36.400 --> 00:22:39.070
as my larger letter.

00:22:39.070 --> 00:22:44.020
But I'm just curious why is
that my error model versus

00:22:44.020 --> 00:22:47.592
an adversarial error who just
picks every small letter?

00:22:47.592 --> 00:22:48.550
PROFESSOR: Great, yeah.

00:22:48.550 --> 00:22:52.360
So the question is,
why is this allowed?

00:22:52.360 --> 00:22:57.080
Why can I just say I decide the
alphabet size is Q to the S?

00:22:57.080 --> 00:22:57.580
Yeah.

00:22:57.580 --> 00:23:00.113
So it depends on the
application and the error model

00:23:00.113 --> 00:23:01.030
that you have in mind.

00:23:01.030 --> 00:23:05.470
So you're absolutely right that
in a lot of different settings,

00:23:05.470 --> 00:23:07.930
the natural error
model acts on bits,

00:23:07.930 --> 00:23:10.330
and this is just not
right for those settings.

00:23:10.330 --> 00:23:14.390
We will see next, I think,
how to actually leverage

00:23:14.390 --> 00:23:15.470
this for those settings.

00:23:15.470 --> 00:23:17.428
But there's another step
that needs to be done.

00:23:17.428 --> 00:23:19.860
However, there are settings
where it makes sense to say,

00:23:19.860 --> 00:23:21.985
oh, the natural unit of
errors is a large alphabet.

00:23:21.985 --> 00:23:24.500
So for example, in distributed
storage, or something,

00:23:24.500 --> 00:23:27.080
you might say, what's
the failure mode?

00:23:27.080 --> 00:23:29.210
That whole server
goes down, right?

00:23:29.210 --> 00:23:32.390
And in that model, a
larger alphabet size

00:23:32.390 --> 00:23:34.716
naturally makes sense.

00:23:34.716 --> 00:23:36.335
STUDENT: That's perfect, thanks.

00:23:36.335 --> 00:23:41.750
STUDENT: In practice, do I just
pick a fixed bipartite balanced

00:23:41.750 --> 00:23:45.050
graph of every size, or is
there some notion of matching

00:23:45.050 --> 00:23:47.422
the graph to the problem?

00:23:47.422 --> 00:23:48.380
PROFESSOR: Great, yeah.

00:23:48.380 --> 00:23:50.660
So the question is, how
do I pick the graph?

00:23:50.660 --> 00:23:52.160
Yeah, so-- yeah.

00:23:52.160 --> 00:23:54.800
So I do need to match this graph
to the problem that I have.

00:23:54.800 --> 00:23:56.150
In particular, what do I need?

00:23:56.150 --> 00:23:58.670
I need the number of
vertices to be the same

00:23:58.670 --> 00:23:59.840
as the number of chunks.

00:23:59.840 --> 00:24:01.310
And I need the
degree of the graph

00:24:01.310 --> 00:24:03.860
to be the size of the chunks.

00:24:03.860 --> 00:24:05.570
And-- yeah.

00:24:05.570 --> 00:24:08.000
Typically, I'm not going to
work through the parameters

00:24:08.000 --> 00:24:08.833
because it's a mess.

00:24:08.833 --> 00:24:12.890
But typically, the number
of chunks is order N,

00:24:12.890 --> 00:24:14.960
and the size of the
chunks is order 1.

00:24:14.960 --> 00:24:19.490
And you-- there exist explicit
constructions of expander

00:24:19.490 --> 00:24:24.797
graphs that for many
such parameter regimes.

00:24:24.797 --> 00:24:26.630
I think it is a fair
critique that you often

00:24:26.630 --> 00:24:29.060
have to take N
fairly large to find

00:24:29.060 --> 00:24:30.990
good constructions of these.

00:24:30.990 --> 00:24:34.495
So, yeah, there's
some happy out there.

00:24:34.495 --> 00:24:35.120
More questions?

00:24:35.120 --> 00:24:35.630
It's really good.

00:24:35.630 --> 00:24:36.130
Yeah.

00:24:36.130 --> 00:24:39.347
STUDENT: In a practical
application, could you--

00:24:39.347 --> 00:24:41.180
to get around the
alphabet size thing, could

00:24:41.180 --> 00:24:43.100
you randomize the
choice of expander graph

00:24:43.100 --> 00:24:46.580
and send that
beforehand and then--

00:24:46.580 --> 00:24:49.070
so that you get around--

00:24:49.070 --> 00:24:51.320
the adversary is not
able to know which--

00:24:51.320 --> 00:24:55.550
where to concentrate its
errors to screw you up, or does

00:24:55.550 --> 00:24:56.550
the question make sense?

00:24:56.550 --> 00:24:57.927
PROFESSOR: Yeah.
So I think the question is--

00:25:00.428 --> 00:25:01.470
there's two things there.

00:25:01.470 --> 00:25:03.575
One, can I just
pick a random graph?

00:25:03.575 --> 00:25:04.950
And the answer is
that we'll have

00:25:04.950 --> 00:25:07.033
this combinatorial property
with high probability.

00:25:07.033 --> 00:25:08.790
So even for a worst
case adversary,

00:25:08.790 --> 00:25:11.140
that will be fine,
with high probability.

00:25:11.140 --> 00:25:15.150
But then, also, can
I do something random

00:25:15.150 --> 00:25:16.770
for the adversary goes.

00:25:16.770 --> 00:25:19.638
STUDENT: Like the alphabet's--
the large alphabet size is

00:25:19.638 --> 00:25:21.930
putting a restriction on the
adversary that it can only

00:25:21.930 --> 00:25:24.455
corrupt within a
block at a time.

00:25:24.455 --> 00:25:25.830
Could you get
around that somehow

00:25:25.830 --> 00:25:27.640
by randomizing the
choice of the graph

00:25:27.640 --> 00:25:30.390
so that it doesn't know
where to concentrate

00:25:30.390 --> 00:25:34.455
its errors to screw you up on
the other side of the graph?

00:25:34.455 --> 00:25:36.970
PROFESSOR: I see.

00:25:36.970 --> 00:25:37.470
Yeah.

00:25:37.470 --> 00:25:39.540
Maybe we can talk more about
that particular suggestion

00:25:39.540 --> 00:25:40.040
offline.

00:25:40.040 --> 00:25:41.610
But in a moment, I
will talk about--

00:25:41.610 --> 00:25:44.400
yeah, I have a whole
chunk coming up

00:25:44.400 --> 00:25:46.620
on how do we turn this
stuff for big alphabets

00:25:46.620 --> 00:25:48.030
into stuff over small
alphabets, and there

00:25:48.030 --> 00:25:49.440
will be some randomness
involved there,

00:25:49.440 --> 00:25:51.540
and maybe that's close to
what you're alluding to.

00:25:51.540 --> 00:25:54.840
So maybe hold that thought
for just a little bit.

00:25:54.840 --> 00:25:56.030
Yeah.

00:25:56.030 --> 00:25:58.570
STUDENT: Is this still-- is
this transformation going

00:25:58.570 --> 00:25:59.770
to maintain the linear--

00:25:59.770 --> 00:26:01.317
the linearity of the code?

00:26:01.317 --> 00:26:03.400
I feel very confused when
I see these blocks being

00:26:03.400 --> 00:26:04.480
broken up and then--

00:26:04.480 --> 00:26:05.560
PROFESSOR: Right, yeah.

00:26:05.560 --> 00:26:07.357
So the question is,
does this maintain

00:26:07.357 --> 00:26:08.440
the linearity of the code?

00:26:08.440 --> 00:26:09.430
Sort of.

00:26:09.430 --> 00:26:12.100
So if my original
code, let's say,

00:26:12.100 --> 00:26:15.850
has these little teeny symbols
that live over some field F,

00:26:15.850 --> 00:26:17.830
and this original
code is F linear,

00:26:17.830 --> 00:26:19.570
then this final
thing is still going

00:26:19.570 --> 00:26:23.260
to be F linear because
shuffled things up.

00:26:23.260 --> 00:26:26.365
But permutations are linear.

00:26:26.365 --> 00:26:27.760
STUDENT: Right, sure.

00:26:27.760 --> 00:26:30.070
PROFESSOR: If your
original code--

00:26:30.070 --> 00:26:31.990
I guess it could
make sense to think

00:26:31.990 --> 00:26:35.050
about this is your original
code and maybe these blocks were

00:26:35.050 --> 00:26:37.090
linear over some extension
field, or something,

00:26:37.090 --> 00:26:38.860
that that will be destroyed.

00:26:38.860 --> 00:26:43.855
But, yeah, linearity over the
small field will be maintained.

00:26:43.855 --> 00:26:44.980
Was there another question?

00:26:44.980 --> 00:26:46.680
That was your question.

00:26:46.680 --> 00:26:47.180
Cool.

00:26:47.180 --> 00:26:47.380
OK.

00:26:47.380 --> 00:26:49.660
So maybe in the interest of
time, let me move forward.

00:26:49.660 --> 00:26:52.360
Hopefully, everyone gets
the basic mechanism here.

00:26:52.360 --> 00:26:57.400
But I want to go back to Henry's
question, which was right--

00:26:57.400 --> 00:26:58.600
here was Reed-Solomon codes.

00:26:58.600 --> 00:26:59.770
I have this little asterisk.

00:26:59.770 --> 00:27:01.060
We could use folded
Reed-Solomon codes.

00:27:01.060 --> 00:27:02.018
What would that get us?

00:27:02.018 --> 00:27:03.760
Well, what we saw the--

00:27:03.760 --> 00:27:05.740
or what we very
quickly waved our hands

00:27:05.740 --> 00:27:07.540
at at the end of
the previous talk

00:27:07.540 --> 00:27:11.290
is that folded Reed-Solomon
codes can do list decoding

00:27:11.290 --> 00:27:12.970
better than Reed-Solomon codes.

00:27:12.970 --> 00:27:15.490
So this whole picture was for--

00:27:15.490 --> 00:27:17.152
list was for unique decoding.

00:27:17.152 --> 00:27:18.610
What if I want to
do list decoding?

00:27:18.610 --> 00:27:20.570
What if there's too much error?

00:27:20.570 --> 00:27:23.410
Then these little Reed-Solomon
codes wouldn't know what to do.

00:27:23.410 --> 00:27:26.350
But if I had a folded
Reed-Solomon code,

00:27:26.350 --> 00:27:28.040
I would know what to do.

00:27:28.040 --> 00:27:29.620
And so this is a
good opportunity

00:27:29.620 --> 00:27:32.000
for a quick aside
on list recovery.

00:27:32.000 --> 00:27:32.500
Yeah.

00:27:32.500 --> 00:27:34.390
So the previous cartoon showed
how to do unique decoding.

00:27:34.390 --> 00:27:35.540
What about list decoding?

00:27:35.540 --> 00:27:36.970
So let's go back
to this cartoon.

00:27:36.970 --> 00:27:38.943
We're halfway through.

00:27:38.943 --> 00:27:40.860
And let's say we want
to do list decoding now.

00:27:40.860 --> 00:27:43.290
So the rate of the inner
code, which should now

00:27:43.290 --> 00:27:45.030
be a folded
Reed-Solomon code here,

00:27:45.030 --> 00:27:48.645
is too high to handle this
amount of error, right?

00:27:48.645 --> 00:27:50.520
So let's say it's a
folded Reed-Solomon code.

00:27:50.520 --> 00:27:54.360
So maybe I can list a code
instead of uniquely decoding.

00:27:54.360 --> 00:27:56.460
And what that's
going to give me is

00:27:56.460 --> 00:28:00.230
a short list of possibilities
for each one of these chunks.

00:28:00.230 --> 00:28:02.330
And most of them are
going to be correct,

00:28:02.330 --> 00:28:04.960
meaning, that the right answer
is contained in those lists.

00:28:04.960 --> 00:28:07.460
And some of them might be wrong
if there are too many errors

00:28:07.460 --> 00:28:10.010
that ended up in that thing.

00:28:10.010 --> 00:28:14.720
And now, we have the problem of,
from this situation going back

00:28:14.720 --> 00:28:18.090
to the original code word
C, how do we do that.

00:28:18.090 --> 00:28:18.590
Right.

00:28:18.590 --> 00:28:20.330
So the problem here
is, we somehow--

00:28:20.330 --> 00:28:24.185
we want to go from these input
lists, this A, B, C, D, E, F,

00:28:24.185 --> 00:28:28.488
F0, Y, Z back to C, or to a
short list of possible C's.

00:28:28.488 --> 00:28:30.530
So we want to generate a
list of all possible C's

00:28:30.530 --> 00:28:31.700
that agree with these lists.

00:28:31.700 --> 00:28:34.310
And this problem is
known as list recovery.

00:28:34.310 --> 00:28:37.310
So here's an informal
definition of list recovery.

00:28:37.310 --> 00:28:39.770
We say that C is
list-recoverable

00:28:39.770 --> 00:28:46.490
if given short input
lists, one for each symbol,

00:28:46.490 --> 00:28:50.720
you can efficiently find
all of the code words in C,

00:28:50.720 --> 00:28:55.190
and implicitly this means that
there are not too many of them,

00:28:55.190 --> 00:28:57.890
so that CI lives in
SI for lots of Is.

00:28:57.890 --> 00:29:00.050
So that's exactly the
problem that we just saw.

00:29:00.050 --> 00:29:02.900
And this problem has a name,
it's called list-recovery.

00:29:02.900 --> 00:29:06.232
Fun exercise-- folded
Reed-Solomon codes actually

00:29:06.232 --> 00:29:07.940
are efficiently
list-recoverable, as well

00:29:07.940 --> 00:29:08.690
as list-decodable.

00:29:08.690 --> 00:29:10.773
I'm not going to work it
out but, hint, we already

00:29:10.773 --> 00:29:11.910
saw the algorithm.

00:29:11.910 --> 00:29:15.050
So if you haven't seen this
before, do it on your own.

00:29:15.050 --> 00:29:18.530
The same Guruswamy-Lang
algorithm will also work.

00:29:18.530 --> 00:29:20.900
OK.

00:29:20.900 --> 00:29:23.570
So what that means for
distance amplification

00:29:23.570 --> 00:29:27.780
is, let's say if we take our
little inner codes to be folded

00:29:27.780 --> 00:29:30.030
Reed-Solomon codes, and we
take that outer code word--

00:29:30.030 --> 00:29:33.270
outer code C to be some
list-recoverable code

00:29:33.270 --> 00:29:35.580
with fast algorithms,
which we'll

00:29:35.580 --> 00:29:39.340
say how to get in a moment,
then if you have a high rate

00:29:39.340 --> 00:29:41.990
code that can handle
a little bit of error,

00:29:41.990 --> 00:29:44.860
then you can transform it
into a rate R code that

00:29:44.860 --> 00:29:47.463
can handle the optimal amount
of error rate R for any R

00:29:47.463 --> 00:29:48.130
that you choose.

00:29:48.130 --> 00:29:50.500
And that's what this
AEL actually gets you.

00:29:50.500 --> 00:29:53.780
So it's a very neat technique.

00:29:53.780 --> 00:29:54.280
Cool.

00:29:54.280 --> 00:29:58.120
Are there questions about
this before I move on to--

00:29:58.120 --> 00:30:01.470
STUDENT: Oh, so in the
definition of list-recovery,

00:30:01.470 --> 00:30:04.530
this S1, S2, et cetera, they
can be overlaps between them?

00:30:04.530 --> 00:30:07.320
Because the way you have showed
us, there were no overlaps.

00:30:07.320 --> 00:30:08.430
PROFESSOR: Oh, yeah,
that's right, yeah.

00:30:08.430 --> 00:30:10.763
These S1, S2, they can have
overlaps between them, yeah.

00:30:10.763 --> 00:30:13.410
I guess I showed A,
B, C, D, whatever,

00:30:13.410 --> 00:30:15.780
just because I didn't
want to do subscripts.

00:30:15.780 --> 00:30:19.010
Yeah, that's right.

00:30:19.010 --> 00:30:20.217
More questions?

00:30:24.790 --> 00:30:25.330
Cool.

00:30:25.330 --> 00:30:26.350
OK.
So where are we?

00:30:26.350 --> 00:30:28.760
So far, we've said--

00:30:28.760 --> 00:30:29.260
all right.

00:30:29.260 --> 00:30:32.110
Technique one,
locality, let's come up

00:30:32.110 --> 00:30:34.120
with a code that has
real fast algorithms

00:30:34.120 --> 00:30:35.870
but real bad parameters.

00:30:35.870 --> 00:30:36.370
All right.

00:30:36.370 --> 00:30:39.010
Distance amplification,
let's transform this code

00:30:39.010 --> 00:30:41.920
to get the parameters
back in line.

00:30:41.920 --> 00:30:43.870
But as several of
you just pointed out,

00:30:43.870 --> 00:30:46.780
like, hey, that makes the
alphabet size real big.

00:30:46.780 --> 00:30:48.130
Why is that allowed?

00:30:48.130 --> 00:30:51.220
Sometimes that's allowed, but
sometimes that's not ideal.

00:30:51.220 --> 00:30:54.550
So our next technique is
going to be two different ways

00:30:54.550 --> 00:30:56.873
to reduce the alphabet size.

00:30:56.873 --> 00:30:59.290
And we're going to start with
code concatenation, which is

00:30:59.290 --> 00:31:03.190
the most natural thing to do.

00:31:03.190 --> 00:31:04.600
So alphabet reduction-- right.

00:31:04.600 --> 00:31:06.250
So so far, all the codes
we've been talking about

00:31:06.250 --> 00:31:07.042
have big alphabets.

00:31:07.042 --> 00:31:08.584
Reed-Solomon codes
need big alphabets

00:31:08.584 --> 00:31:10.917
because they need the field
size to be at least a number

00:31:10.917 --> 00:31:11.770
of valuation points.

00:31:11.770 --> 00:31:15.400
Folded Reed-Solomon codes
have alphabet size even bigger

00:31:15.400 --> 00:31:17.350
because we did the folding, AEL.

00:31:17.350 --> 00:31:19.210
This distance
amplification we just saw

00:31:19.210 --> 00:31:22.720
might make the alphabet
size yet bigger still.

00:31:22.720 --> 00:31:24.680
Again, this is fine
for some applications,

00:31:24.680 --> 00:31:29.245
but ideally, we'd want binary
codes for many applications.

00:31:29.245 --> 00:31:30.620
I just want to
take a quick aside

00:31:30.620 --> 00:31:33.110
to mention that
this is a challenge.

00:31:33.110 --> 00:31:35.570
So lots and lots of big
open problems whenever

00:31:35.570 --> 00:31:37.520
binary codes are involved.

00:31:37.520 --> 00:31:39.170
So here are some
natural questions

00:31:39.170 --> 00:31:41.390
you might want to ask
about binary codes.

00:31:41.390 --> 00:31:43.820
What is the best trade-off
between rate and distance

00:31:43.820 --> 00:31:45.830
possible for binary codes?

00:31:45.830 --> 00:31:48.080
Open, right, this is one of
the biggest open questions

00:31:48.080 --> 00:31:50.090
in coding theory.

00:31:50.090 --> 00:31:53.547
How about the best trade-off
that we know to exist?

00:31:53.547 --> 00:31:55.880
So this is something called
the Gilbert-Varshamov bound.

00:31:55.880 --> 00:31:58.400
It is attained by a
random linear code.

00:31:58.400 --> 00:32:00.060
Looks something like this.

00:32:00.060 --> 00:32:00.560
Right.

00:32:00.560 --> 00:32:04.850
So basically, as the distance,
the relative distance

00:32:04.850 --> 00:32:06.530
goes from zero to a half.

00:32:06.530 --> 00:32:08.180
So for binary codes,
the best distance

00:32:08.180 --> 00:32:11.270
you can really hope
for is like a half.

00:32:11.270 --> 00:32:16.050
So by binary codes, I just mean
that the alphabet is zero, 1.

00:32:16.050 --> 00:32:16.550
Yeah.

00:32:16.550 --> 00:32:19.100
So as we go from zero
to a half, the best--

00:32:19.100 --> 00:32:22.760
or the best rate we know how to
get decreases something like 1

00:32:22.760 --> 00:32:25.430
minus the binary
entropy of delta.

00:32:25.430 --> 00:32:26.900
This is not that curve.

00:32:26.900 --> 00:32:29.240
This is a curve that
I drew in PowerPoint.

00:32:29.240 --> 00:32:32.240
But the way to think
about it, at least,

00:32:32.240 --> 00:32:35.570
down in this parameter regime
is that if you back off

00:32:35.570 --> 00:32:38.010
from this point by a half--

00:32:38.010 --> 00:32:38.510
sorry.

00:32:38.510 --> 00:32:40.400
If you back off from a
half by a little bit,

00:32:40.400 --> 00:32:42.740
let's say, by epsilon, then
if you take a Taylor expansion

00:32:42.740 --> 00:32:44.540
of this, you can see that
the rate that you should get

00:32:44.540 --> 00:32:46.670
is something on the
order of epsilon squared.

00:32:46.670 --> 00:32:53.040
So close to this point, this is
roughly looks like a problem.

00:32:53.040 --> 00:32:53.540
OK.

00:32:53.540 --> 00:32:54.860
So-- right.

00:32:54.860 --> 00:32:56.602
At least this one I
have an answer for.

00:32:56.602 --> 00:32:58.310
What's the best
trade-off we know exists?

00:32:58.310 --> 00:33:00.230
It's this
Gilbert-Varshamov bound.

00:33:00.230 --> 00:33:01.040
Can we do better?

00:33:01.040 --> 00:33:02.210
Open question.

00:33:02.210 --> 00:33:03.420
However, we can ask--

00:33:03.420 --> 00:33:03.920
OK.

00:33:03.920 --> 00:33:05.420
So we know that
these codes exist.

00:33:05.420 --> 00:33:08.000
Can we construct them, right?

00:33:08.000 --> 00:33:11.390
This is also largely
a big open question.

00:33:11.390 --> 00:33:13.100
So there's been really
exciting progress

00:33:13.100 --> 00:33:14.640
on this in the past 5, 10 years.

00:33:14.640 --> 00:33:16.310
So for example,
Ta-Shma's code, which

00:33:16.310 --> 00:33:21.920
were a big breakthrough
in 2015-2016 and related

00:33:21.920 --> 00:33:23.810
constructions,
these can get rate

00:33:23.810 --> 00:33:25.940
very close to the
Gilbert-Varshamov bound

00:33:25.940 --> 00:33:29.150
in this parameter regime where
we're real close to a hack.

00:33:29.150 --> 00:33:31.610
So we now do have explicit
constructions of codes there.

00:33:31.610 --> 00:33:35.300
But in other parameter
regimes, a big open question.

00:33:35.300 --> 00:33:38.510
And you might ask, OK,
how about algorithms

00:33:38.510 --> 00:33:40.545
for those explicit codes?

00:33:40.545 --> 00:33:42.170
So we-- actually, we
do have algorithms

00:33:42.170 --> 00:33:44.120
for Ta-Shma codes near
linear time algorithms,

00:33:44.120 --> 00:33:46.410
which are very exciting.

00:33:46.410 --> 00:33:49.490
So for example, these works by
Jeronimo, Srivastava, Tulsani,

00:33:49.490 --> 00:33:52.747
and also Blanc and Doron for
related code construction,

00:33:52.747 --> 00:33:53.705
these are near linear--

00:33:56.560 --> 00:33:58.480
yeah, near linear
algorithms for these codes.

00:33:58.480 --> 00:34:00.277
But again-- for
these explicit codes,

00:34:00.277 --> 00:34:02.860
but again, that's sort of only
in this very low rate parameter

00:34:02.860 --> 00:34:03.360
regime.

00:34:03.360 --> 00:34:06.220
So there's tons of
open questions here.

00:34:06.220 --> 00:34:09.389
What is the right answer,
and how do we get it?

00:34:09.389 --> 00:34:11.860
These very, very
general questions.

00:34:11.860 --> 00:34:14.780
So there's huge challenges
there with binary codes.

00:34:14.780 --> 00:34:15.280
OK.

00:34:15.280 --> 00:34:17.739
So with that backdrop,
let me tell you

00:34:17.739 --> 00:34:21.460
how we can get close to binary.

00:34:21.460 --> 00:34:21.960
Cool.

00:34:21.960 --> 00:34:22.170
OK.

00:34:22.170 --> 00:34:23.730
So the plan for alphabet
reduction techniques

00:34:23.730 --> 00:34:25.290
is, first, I'll talk
about code concatenation,

00:34:25.290 --> 00:34:27.082
and then I'll talk
very, very, very quickly

00:34:27.082 --> 00:34:28.530
about algebraic geometry codes.

00:34:28.530 --> 00:34:32.760
And then we'll see an aside,
which this is probably

00:34:32.760 --> 00:34:34.710
where the talk is going to end.

00:34:34.710 --> 00:34:36.389
What is all this get us?

00:34:36.389 --> 00:34:38.520
I'm throwing these
techniques at you,

00:34:38.520 --> 00:34:41.042
what happens when you actually
use them all together?

00:34:41.042 --> 00:34:42.750
What can you get, and
what can't you get?

00:34:42.750 --> 00:34:45.130
And that's what I'll do.

00:34:45.130 --> 00:34:45.630
OK.

00:34:45.630 --> 00:34:48.750
But first, let's see this
technique code concatenation.

00:34:48.750 --> 00:34:50.250
So this is the first
thing you would

00:34:50.250 --> 00:34:53.130
try to do when you have a
code over a big alphabet

00:34:53.130 --> 00:34:55.800
and you want it to be
over a small alphabet.

00:34:55.800 --> 00:34:58.260
Basically, you take
your code word,

00:34:58.260 --> 00:35:00.835
so it has all of these symbols
that live in a big alphabet.

00:35:00.835 --> 00:35:02.460
And basically, what
I'm going to do is,

00:35:02.460 --> 00:35:05.040
I'm going to treat each
symbol as a little message.

00:35:05.040 --> 00:35:07.590
And I'm going to encode it
with a little binary code.

00:35:07.590 --> 00:35:11.610
So let's say that C prime
is some little binary code.

00:35:11.610 --> 00:35:16.050
I'm going to take C1, the first
symbol here of my code word,

00:35:16.050 --> 00:35:18.090
and encode that
with my code C prime

00:35:18.090 --> 00:35:19.620
to get a little binary vector.

00:35:19.620 --> 00:35:21.130
And I'll do that
for all of them.

00:35:21.130 --> 00:35:24.430
And then I'm going to get a
final code word that is binary.

00:35:24.430 --> 00:35:27.160
It lives in zero 1
to the something.

00:35:27.160 --> 00:35:28.930
Make sense?

00:35:28.930 --> 00:35:29.830
Cool.

00:35:29.830 --> 00:35:30.430
OK.

00:35:30.430 --> 00:35:34.840
So algorithmically,
this seems great, right,

00:35:34.840 --> 00:35:38.320
in terms of decoding
algorithms, right?

00:35:38.320 --> 00:35:43.330
Even if I don't have any
explicit constructions, or even

00:35:43.330 --> 00:35:45.580
any good algorithms
in the parameters

00:35:45.580 --> 00:35:47.980
that I want for these
binary code C prime.

00:35:47.980 --> 00:35:51.130
If they're small
enough, then brute force

00:35:51.130 --> 00:35:53.410
counts as efficient, right.

00:35:53.410 --> 00:35:54.550
So, OK.

00:35:54.550 --> 00:35:56.290
I see some people
being theorists.

00:35:56.290 --> 00:35:58.030
Yes, I know.

00:35:58.030 --> 00:35:59.420
Yeah.

00:35:59.420 --> 00:35:59.950
Yes.

00:35:59.950 --> 00:36:04.630
Oh, good, even to a theorist
open problem make that better.

00:36:04.630 --> 00:36:06.280
Yeah, OK.

00:36:06.280 --> 00:36:07.510
Good.

00:36:07.510 --> 00:36:08.680
Yeah.

00:36:08.680 --> 00:36:12.530
But combinatorially, it's not
so great even for a theorist,

00:36:12.530 --> 00:36:13.030
right?

00:36:13.030 --> 00:36:17.668
Because the final-- so when you
concatenate, what happens is,

00:36:17.668 --> 00:36:19.210
basically, you
multiply the two rates

00:36:19.210 --> 00:36:20.810
and you multiply
the two distances.

00:36:20.810 --> 00:36:23.290
So even if-- what that
means is that even

00:36:23.290 --> 00:36:26.020
if your original codes, both the
outer code and the inner code

00:36:26.020 --> 00:36:27.190
that you start
with, are optimal,

00:36:27.190 --> 00:36:28.773
their composition
is not going to be--

00:36:28.773 --> 00:36:30.920
or the concatenation
is not going to be.

00:36:30.920 --> 00:36:34.370
So we won't be able to handle
the right amount of worst case

00:36:34.370 --> 00:36:35.730
errors if we wanted to do this.

00:36:35.730 --> 00:36:41.150
So side note, actually,
this can optimally,

00:36:41.150 --> 00:36:45.110
for some flavors of theorists,
handle random errors

00:36:45.110 --> 00:36:46.210
for concatenated codes.

00:36:46.210 --> 00:36:47.210
That's a different talk.

00:36:47.210 --> 00:36:49.580
But for worst case
errors, this is not great.

00:36:49.580 --> 00:36:51.230
So instead, what I
want to talk about

00:36:51.230 --> 00:36:53.420
is a technique due to Thommesen.

00:36:53.420 --> 00:36:54.830
And the basic idea
here, and this

00:36:54.830 --> 00:36:57.038
is maybe close to the question
you were asking about,

00:36:57.038 --> 00:36:59.000
is what I'm going
to do is I'm going

00:36:59.000 --> 00:37:02.780
to choose different inner
codes for each position.

00:37:02.780 --> 00:37:04.910
I'm going to choose
them randomly.

00:37:04.910 --> 00:37:07.820
So just random linear codes iid.

00:37:07.820 --> 00:37:10.350
So I'll start out with
my outer code word here.

00:37:10.350 --> 00:37:13.010
And I'm going to concatenate it.

00:37:13.010 --> 00:37:17.000
The first symbol is going to be
encoded by a random linear code

00:37:17.000 --> 00:37:17.853
C1 prime.

00:37:17.853 --> 00:37:19.520
The second symbol is
going to be encoded

00:37:19.520 --> 00:37:23.588
with the random linear
code C2 prime, and so on.

00:37:23.588 --> 00:37:25.130
So this is a randomized
construction,

00:37:25.130 --> 00:37:26.953
but Thommesen, in
the '80s, showed

00:37:26.953 --> 00:37:29.370
that this actually approaches
the Gilbert-Varshamov bound.

00:37:29.370 --> 00:37:32.090
So this attains-- we don't
know if it's optimal,

00:37:32.090 --> 00:37:33.680
but the best
trade-off we know how

00:37:33.680 --> 00:37:37.580
to attain for binary codes
between rate and distance.

00:37:37.580 --> 00:37:39.170
That's pretty cool.

00:37:39.170 --> 00:37:42.350
And moreover, the thing
I want to talk about now

00:37:42.350 --> 00:37:45.500
is, actually, if the outer
code is list-recoverable,

00:37:45.500 --> 00:37:48.170
which we just defined and
also is in the right parameter

00:37:48.170 --> 00:37:52.002
regime, then not only does
this have optimal trade-offs,

00:37:52.002 --> 00:37:53.960
but there are efficient
algorithms to decode it

00:37:53.960 --> 00:37:55.400
to half the distance.

00:37:55.400 --> 00:37:58.217
So we said that we didn't--

00:37:58.217 --> 00:37:59.800
even though it's a
really hard problem

00:37:59.800 --> 00:38:03.850
to come up with explicit
constructions of these codes,

00:38:03.850 --> 00:38:06.190
with or without efficient
algorithms, actually,

00:38:06.190 --> 00:38:07.660
there are randomized
constructions

00:38:07.660 --> 00:38:10.060
of these codes that do
have efficient algorithms.

00:38:10.060 --> 00:38:12.970
STUDENT: What is the outer code
for Thommesen's construction

00:38:12.970 --> 00:38:14.550
approaches?

00:38:14.550 --> 00:38:16.600
PROFESSOR: Any MDS code.

00:38:16.600 --> 00:38:18.520
STUDENT: Yes, Reed-Solomon
code, for example.

00:38:21.520 --> 00:38:22.290
PROFESSOR: Yeah.

00:38:22.290 --> 00:38:23.714
All it needs is--

00:38:23.714 --> 00:38:26.500
Yeah, any of these.

00:38:26.500 --> 00:38:30.660
Other-- yeah.

00:38:30.660 --> 00:38:32.160
STUDENT: Could you
give me intuition

00:38:32.160 --> 00:38:36.930
for why in this case with
the randomized construction,

00:38:36.930 --> 00:38:39.570
we don't end up
losing on the rate,

00:38:39.570 --> 00:38:43.962
like why the optimality
play out and get--

00:38:43.962 --> 00:38:44.670
PROFESSOR: Right.

00:38:44.670 --> 00:38:47.520
So the rates do multiply,
that's still true.

00:38:47.520 --> 00:38:50.580
With distance, I
guess the reason

00:38:50.580 --> 00:38:54.200
you multiply the distance
in the other thing, like,

00:38:54.200 --> 00:38:55.950
you're in the analysis
and the other thing

00:38:55.950 --> 00:39:01.770
is that it's sort of a very
worst case type of notion.

00:39:01.770 --> 00:39:06.210
The thing that can go wrong
is that if the adversary

00:39:06.210 --> 00:39:09.690
chooses to clobber
each of the symbols

00:39:09.690 --> 00:39:13.160
just enough to make them--

00:39:13.160 --> 00:39:15.820
yeah, like, she chooses to
concentrate their errors

00:39:15.820 --> 00:39:18.663
just right, and it
can throw things off.

00:39:18.663 --> 00:39:20.080
Sorry, I'm not
being very precise.

00:39:20.080 --> 00:39:21.295
But, yeah.

00:39:21.295 --> 00:39:22.490
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE].

00:39:22.490 --> 00:39:23.390
PROFESSOR: Yeah, OK.

00:39:23.390 --> 00:39:23.890
Yeah.

00:39:23.890 --> 00:39:26.813
And then somehow
this gets around it.

00:39:26.813 --> 00:39:27.730
This gets around that.

00:39:27.730 --> 00:39:31.000
STUDENT: And so is the idea
that the adversary doesn't

00:39:31.000 --> 00:39:33.760
know your randomization, doesn't
know your random coins, or--

00:39:33.760 --> 00:39:36.093
PROFESSOR: No, the adversary
does know the random coins.

00:39:36.093 --> 00:39:37.700
This is still a
worst case statement.

00:39:43.180 --> 00:39:43.680
Yeah.

00:39:43.680 --> 00:39:44.715
We can take this
discussion off line.

00:39:44.715 --> 00:39:45.720
I don't have--

00:39:45.720 --> 00:39:48.315
I have very technical
intuition, which

00:39:48.315 --> 00:39:50.190
won't make sense when
it comes out of my face

00:39:50.190 --> 00:39:51.357
without a whiteboard marker.

00:39:51.357 --> 00:39:52.710
Yeah.

00:39:52.710 --> 00:39:55.350
Does anyone have
good short intuition?

00:40:00.587 --> 00:40:02.420
Like the analysis works
and then it doesn't?

00:40:02.420 --> 00:40:05.062
Yeah, yeah, both of these
are not too hard to show,

00:40:05.062 --> 00:40:06.770
so I can maybe walk
you through it later.

00:40:06.770 --> 00:40:07.940
Yeah.

00:40:07.940 --> 00:40:09.270
Cool.

00:40:09.270 --> 00:40:09.770
OK.

00:40:09.770 --> 00:40:10.270
Yeah.

00:40:10.270 --> 00:40:14.180
But what I'd like to do
now is give some intuition

00:40:14.180 --> 00:40:17.690
about why this is the case,
because this should motivate--

00:40:17.690 --> 00:40:19.700
lots of people were
asking when I was like,

00:40:19.700 --> 00:40:23.940
look, we get these really high
rate codes with fast algorithms

00:40:23.940 --> 00:40:26.550
over enormous alphabets
that are list-recoverable.

00:40:26.550 --> 00:40:27.425
Why do we care?

00:40:27.425 --> 00:40:29.550
This is why-- this is one
reason why we might care.

00:40:29.550 --> 00:40:34.200
So let me say how you do this.

00:40:34.200 --> 00:40:38.797
So suppose that-- so let's take
for granted this result that

00:40:38.797 --> 00:40:40.880
these things approach the
Gilbert-Varshamov bound,

00:40:40.880 --> 00:40:42.890
and let's say that T--

00:40:42.890 --> 00:40:46.190
the errors are as close
to N times delta over 2.

00:40:46.190 --> 00:40:48.710
So we have close to
half the distance,

00:40:48.710 --> 00:40:49.950
and we want to correct it.

00:40:49.950 --> 00:40:50.450
All right.

00:40:50.450 --> 00:40:52.100
So what does that look like?

00:40:52.100 --> 00:40:54.110
So here's our concatenated code.

00:40:54.110 --> 00:40:57.890
And in our picture, we're going
to have our geometry picture

00:40:57.890 --> 00:40:59.600
over here that we keep
having if we want.

00:40:59.600 --> 00:41:01.760
And then a bunch of
errors get introduced,

00:41:01.760 --> 00:41:04.120
and they get introduced
however the adversary

00:41:04.120 --> 00:41:05.120
wants to introduce them.

00:41:05.120 --> 00:41:07.370
So maybe this whole symbol
gets clobbered or something

00:41:07.370 --> 00:41:08.540
like that.

00:41:08.540 --> 00:41:11.060
And here what that
looks like is this.

00:41:11.060 --> 00:41:13.820
So we know that the
code combinatorially

00:41:13.820 --> 00:41:17.030
is uniquely decodable for
this fraction of errors.

00:41:17.030 --> 00:41:18.920
So we know that there
is a unique code

00:41:18.920 --> 00:41:21.593
word that is T close to it.

00:41:21.593 --> 00:41:22.760
And we just want to find it.

00:41:22.760 --> 00:41:24.350
So what are we going to do?

00:41:24.350 --> 00:41:26.210
Here's the algorithm.

00:41:26.210 --> 00:41:29.360
And I don't have a
citation on this slide?

00:41:29.360 --> 00:41:32.930
I think this is first
due to an Indic idea,

00:41:32.930 --> 00:41:34.760
and then has been
built on since.

00:41:34.760 --> 00:41:38.760
The basic idea is,
let's list a code,

00:41:38.760 --> 00:41:40.703
each inner code by brute force.

00:41:40.703 --> 00:41:42.870
Again, because they are so
short and I'm a theorist.

00:41:42.870 --> 00:41:45.210
I'm going to call
this efficient.

00:41:45.210 --> 00:41:47.580
And now, what I'm
going to get is

00:41:47.580 --> 00:41:51.870
a set of possible symbols for
each one of these outer code

00:41:51.870 --> 00:41:54.250
symbols here, right.

00:41:54.250 --> 00:41:56.380
And again, to
Lolita's point, I'm

00:41:56.380 --> 00:41:59.260
just being sloppy by
drawing these as distinct.

00:41:59.260 --> 00:42:03.630
They could be arbitrary
with whatever we have.

00:42:03.630 --> 00:42:04.130
Cool.

00:42:04.130 --> 00:42:06.020
And then this is--

00:42:06.020 --> 00:42:07.838
now, we can use our
list-recovery algorithm

00:42:07.838 --> 00:42:08.630
for the outer coat.

00:42:08.630 --> 00:42:11.240
I have a short list of
possibilities for each symbol.

00:42:11.240 --> 00:42:14.840
And I have this, supposedly
now, this outer code

00:42:14.840 --> 00:42:15.920
that is list-recoverable.

00:42:15.920 --> 00:42:16.657
So let's do that.

00:42:16.657 --> 00:42:18.740
And now I get a short list
of possible code words.

00:42:18.740 --> 00:42:22.010
So in this picture, maybe that
short list looks like this.

00:42:22.010 --> 00:42:24.830
But combinatorially, we know
that only one of them is close.

00:42:24.830 --> 00:42:27.440
And if I have a short list,
even a polynomial sized list,

00:42:27.440 --> 00:42:29.720
that still gives me an
efficient algorithm, right?

00:42:29.720 --> 00:42:32.120
I take this short list,
I check everything in it,

00:42:32.120 --> 00:42:34.430
I pick the one
correct answer, right?

00:42:34.430 --> 00:42:36.530
So by tumescence
result, at most,

00:42:36.530 --> 00:42:38.060
one of these things
in this list is

00:42:38.060 --> 00:42:39.920
close to the receive word,
just try them all and find it.

00:42:39.920 --> 00:42:41.587
And that gives us an
efficient algorithm

00:42:41.587 --> 00:42:43.880
to uniquely decode
this concatenated code,

00:42:43.880 --> 00:42:47.210
as long as I have an efficient
algorithm to list decode,

00:42:47.210 --> 00:42:50.325
or list recover the outer code.

00:42:50.325 --> 00:42:51.200
Does that make sense?

00:42:56.140 --> 00:42:56.650
Cool.

00:42:56.650 --> 00:42:57.150
OK.

00:42:57.150 --> 00:42:58.780
So punch line from
code concatenation

00:42:58.780 --> 00:43:00.520
here is that even
though it's open,

00:43:00.520 --> 00:43:02.410
it's a big open
problem to construct

00:43:02.410 --> 00:43:05.290
explicit codes of rate,
maybe bounded away

00:43:05.290 --> 00:43:07.690
from zero that
approach the GV bound.

00:43:07.690 --> 00:43:10.540
We can get randomized
constructions of these codes

00:43:10.540 --> 00:43:12.900
and decode them efficiently.

00:43:12.900 --> 00:43:15.390
And if you're interested
in parameters,

00:43:15.390 --> 00:43:17.460
hold on for just a
few slides and I'll

00:43:17.460 --> 00:43:20.330
get back to that in a second.

00:43:20.330 --> 00:43:20.830
OK.

00:43:20.830 --> 00:43:22.100
So how long do I have?

00:43:22.100 --> 00:43:24.310
10, 15 minutes?

00:43:24.310 --> 00:43:25.120
Yeah.

00:43:25.120 --> 00:43:25.840
OK, cool.

00:43:25.840 --> 00:43:30.340
So let me very quickly say one
other technique for alphabet

00:43:30.340 --> 00:43:34.240
reduction, and that's using
algebraic geometry codes.

00:43:34.240 --> 00:43:38.320
So as a motivating
question, we might ask, OK,

00:43:38.320 --> 00:43:40.750
why do Reed-Solomon codes
have such big alphabets?

00:43:40.750 --> 00:43:43.437
Well, it's because we needed
lots of different evaluation

00:43:43.437 --> 00:43:44.020
points, right?

00:43:44.020 --> 00:43:45.880
If I have just one
evaluation point

00:43:45.880 --> 00:43:48.202
and I evaluate my polynomial
at all of those points,

00:43:48.202 --> 00:43:50.410
this does not give me a very
interesting code, right?

00:43:50.410 --> 00:43:51.785
This is just the
repetition code.

00:43:51.785 --> 00:43:53.615
So we need distinct
evaluation points.

00:43:53.615 --> 00:43:55.240
And so we ask ourselves,
well, is there

00:43:55.240 --> 00:43:57.370
some way we can
get more evaluation

00:43:57.370 --> 00:44:00.700
points without making
the field size larger?

00:44:00.700 --> 00:44:04.060
This is very basic
idea of codes.

00:44:04.060 --> 00:44:06.163
Huge disclaimer, I am
so far from an expert

00:44:06.163 --> 00:44:07.330
in algebraic geometry codes.

00:44:07.330 --> 00:44:09.910
There are much-- people who
have much more expertise

00:44:09.910 --> 00:44:10.840
in this room.

00:44:10.840 --> 00:44:13.420
But here's my real
quick picture.

00:44:13.420 --> 00:44:15.160
So take some nice
algebraic curve,

00:44:15.160 --> 00:44:16.750
call it curly X.
This is my picture

00:44:16.750 --> 00:44:19.870
of a nice algebraic curve
as rendered in PowerPoint.

00:44:19.870 --> 00:44:22.300
Let's look at all of
the points on curly X

00:44:22.300 --> 00:44:28.090
that lie in the
line FQ at the end.

00:44:28.090 --> 00:44:31.780
And the hope is so many
points, way more than Q points,

00:44:31.780 --> 00:44:36.352
if our curve is nice and curvy,
got lots of points on it.

00:44:36.352 --> 00:44:38.560
Then we're going to take
our set of functions curly F

00:44:38.560 --> 00:44:43.370
to be some analog of low degree
polynomials on that curve.

00:44:43.370 --> 00:44:45.190
So I think, formally,
you pick a bunch

00:44:45.190 --> 00:44:47.110
of functions in some
Riemann-Roch space,

00:44:47.110 --> 00:44:51.310
and then we're going to define
our evaluation code as usual.

00:44:51.310 --> 00:44:53.560
So take all of
our functions that

00:44:53.560 --> 00:44:57.250
live in curly F evaluated
all on these points in P.

00:44:57.250 --> 00:45:00.768
And now, hooray, we can take
the field size to be smaller

00:45:00.768 --> 00:45:02.560
because we got all of
these points for free

00:45:02.560 --> 00:45:03.950
because we use this cool curve.

00:45:03.950 --> 00:45:07.750
So punch lines are that you
can get Reed-Solomon like codes

00:45:07.750 --> 00:45:09.850
over constant size
alphabets that are still

00:45:09.850 --> 00:45:13.930
amenable to this basic paradigm
that we saw in the first talk,

00:45:13.930 --> 00:45:17.590
interpolate some
polynomial constants.

00:45:17.590 --> 00:45:20.240
And, in fact, you
can even get codes

00:45:20.240 --> 00:45:21.770
over constant size
alphabets that

00:45:21.770 --> 00:45:22.970
actually beat the GV bound.

00:45:22.970 --> 00:45:25.040
You just can't get binary codes.

00:45:25.040 --> 00:45:28.010
But there are codes
over, let's say, F 49

00:45:28.010 --> 00:45:30.680
but you need the GV bound.

00:45:30.680 --> 00:45:31.200
Cool.

00:45:31.200 --> 00:45:31.700
OK.

00:45:31.700 --> 00:45:32.970
That's all I'm going
to say about algebraic.

00:45:32.970 --> 00:45:34.760
How many codes, ask
a mathematician.

00:45:34.760 --> 00:45:36.560
So I'll tell you more.

00:45:36.560 --> 00:45:37.100
Great.

00:45:37.100 --> 00:45:39.680
But what I wanted to
do to wrap up the talk

00:45:39.680 --> 00:45:43.310
is talk about what do all
of these techniques get us?

00:45:43.310 --> 00:45:45.590
So I've given you a
bunch of different--

00:45:45.590 --> 00:45:47.840
or I've told you about a
bunch of different techniques

00:45:47.840 --> 00:45:50.600
that we can try to use
on top of this framework

00:45:50.600 --> 00:45:54.350
of interpolated polynomial
and find some roots.

00:45:54.350 --> 00:45:55.490
What happens when we do it?

00:45:55.490 --> 00:45:55.990
OK.

00:45:55.990 --> 00:45:57.268
So here's just one example.

00:45:57.268 --> 00:45:59.810
So these techniques have been
used in lots and lots of papers

00:45:59.810 --> 00:46:02.213
altogether, but I'm just
going to pick one example.

00:46:02.213 --> 00:46:04.130
This is a paper by Brett
Hemenway, Noga Ramzi,

00:46:04.130 --> 00:46:06.830
and myself from five
years ago, or something.

00:46:06.830 --> 00:46:09.095
And this uses, literally,
all of the techniques

00:46:09.095 --> 00:46:10.970
we've seen so far on
top of each other, which

00:46:10.970 --> 00:46:12.420
is why I picked this one.

00:46:12.420 --> 00:46:12.920
OK.

00:46:12.920 --> 00:46:15.770
And so here's, basically,
what this paper does.

00:46:15.770 --> 00:46:18.050
You start with an
algebraic geometry

00:46:18.050 --> 00:46:19.940
code that is list-recoverable.

00:46:19.940 --> 00:46:21.860
So here's technique
3B that we just talked

00:46:21.860 --> 00:46:23.152
about algebraic geometry codes.

00:46:23.152 --> 00:46:25.910
So we used one that's due
to Guruswamy and Xing.

00:46:25.910 --> 00:46:28.850
And then we used this basic
interpolated polynomial

00:46:28.850 --> 00:46:31.010
and find the roots
paradigm to come up

00:46:31.010 --> 00:46:32.460
with a list-recovery algorithm.

00:46:32.460 --> 00:46:32.960
OK.

00:46:32.960 --> 00:46:37.850
We have this, but it's
slow, the algorithm is slow.

00:46:37.850 --> 00:46:38.900
So what do we do?

00:46:38.900 --> 00:46:40.235
We use technique one, locality.

00:46:40.235 --> 00:46:42.980
We take a big tensor
product, tensor it up.

00:46:42.980 --> 00:46:45.710
And what we end up with
is a high rate code

00:46:45.710 --> 00:46:48.230
with fast list-recovery, but
really terrible distance.

00:46:48.230 --> 00:46:50.340
So it can't handle very
many errors at all.

00:46:50.340 --> 00:46:50.840
OK.

00:46:50.840 --> 00:46:51.632
What do we do next?

00:46:51.632 --> 00:46:53.948
We do this AEL
distance amplification,

00:46:53.948 --> 00:46:56.240
and that's going to give us
an optimally list decodable

00:46:56.240 --> 00:47:00.185
code with fast algorithms
over a real big alphabet using

00:47:00.185 --> 00:47:01.310
that technique that we saw.

00:47:01.310 --> 00:47:03.823
And then we can concatenate
and do this tumescent technique

00:47:03.823 --> 00:47:05.240
that we just saw,
and that's going

00:47:05.240 --> 00:47:07.910
to give us a randomized
construction of codes

00:47:07.910 --> 00:47:10.640
approaching the GV bound
with fast algorithms.

00:47:10.640 --> 00:47:12.470
You can do that all together.

00:47:12.470 --> 00:47:15.860
And quantitatively, the punch
lines that you get are--

00:47:15.860 --> 00:47:17.750
so if we're interested
in list decodable

00:47:17.750 --> 00:47:19.850
or list-recoverable codes,
that's this box here.

00:47:19.850 --> 00:47:22.520
We can get list decodable
codes with any rate R

00:47:22.520 --> 00:47:26.540
that you want, radius
approaching optimal.

00:47:26.540 --> 00:47:29.150
So N times 1 minus R, if you
remember from the first talk

00:47:29.150 --> 00:47:30.530
this is what we wanted.

00:47:30.530 --> 00:47:36.230
Alphabet size, very close to
linear in N. Decoding time,

00:47:36.230 --> 00:47:41.180
very close to linear in N.
And list size sublinear in N.

00:47:41.180 --> 00:47:45.140
The punchlines-- and if you
care about binary codes, what

00:47:45.140 --> 00:47:47.690
you can get is any rate.

00:47:47.690 --> 00:47:50.270
OK, it can't be too big, but
at least it's-- can be as big

00:47:50.270 --> 00:47:53.270
as a constant, so
anything less than 0.02,

00:47:53.270 --> 00:47:56.073
and distance delta
with approaching

00:47:56.073 --> 00:47:57.240
the Gilbert-Varshamov bound.

00:47:57.240 --> 00:47:59.532
So the rate is approximately
1 minus the binary entropy

00:47:59.532 --> 00:48:00.620
of delta.

00:48:00.620 --> 00:48:02.810
And it has unique
decoding algorithms

00:48:02.810 --> 00:48:07.770
up to half the distance running
in time near linear N to the 1.

00:48:07.770 --> 00:48:09.330
So these are all
the punch lines.

00:48:09.330 --> 00:48:11.622
If I describe it like this,
it's like, well, everything

00:48:11.622 --> 00:48:12.870
is solved, isn't it?

00:48:12.870 --> 00:48:17.880
But these are very rose-tinted
glasses punch lines.

00:48:17.880 --> 00:48:21.270
So what I want to
do in the next slide

00:48:21.270 --> 00:48:25.800
is maybe take some of
the rose tinting off

00:48:25.800 --> 00:48:28.860
and talk about some open
questions that are left here,

00:48:28.860 --> 00:48:30.180
a.k.a., the dirty truth.

00:48:30.180 --> 00:48:36.547
And, yeah, so the dirty truth
is that, basically, each time we

00:48:36.547 --> 00:48:38.130
apply one of these
techniques, they're

00:48:38.130 --> 00:48:39.780
very cool and very powerful.

00:48:39.780 --> 00:48:41.347
But every time we
apply one of them,

00:48:41.347 --> 00:48:42.930
the dependence on
some other parameter

00:48:42.930 --> 00:48:45.900
that we don't like to talk
about typically gets worse.

00:48:45.900 --> 00:48:48.810
So for example, for all of those
glowing punch lines that I said

00:48:48.810 --> 00:48:52.692
on the previous slide, don't
ask me what the constants are,

00:48:52.692 --> 00:48:54.150
don't ask me how
big N needs to be,

00:48:54.150 --> 00:48:55.942
and please don't ask
me what the dependence

00:48:55.942 --> 00:48:57.240
on the gap to capacity is.

00:48:57.240 --> 00:48:59.700
So if the rate is
going to be epsilon off

00:48:59.700 --> 00:49:02.640
from the
Gilbert-Varshamov bound,

00:49:02.640 --> 00:49:04.570
what's the dependence
on epsilon?

00:49:04.570 --> 00:49:05.070
OK.

00:49:05.070 --> 00:49:07.310
So you can ask me, I will
tell you, it's in the paper,

00:49:07.310 --> 00:49:08.018
we worked it out.

00:49:08.018 --> 00:49:10.420
But it's embarrassing, right?

00:49:10.420 --> 00:49:12.100
And so I think--

00:49:12.100 --> 00:49:14.620
and I think that
the reason that I

00:49:14.620 --> 00:49:18.790
don't want to answer these
questions in public is,

00:49:18.790 --> 00:49:20.590
the reason is because
I'm a classy person.

00:49:20.590 --> 00:49:25.330
But the technical reason is
because, yeah, basically,

00:49:25.330 --> 00:49:28.270
every time we apply one
of these techniques,

00:49:28.270 --> 00:49:32.710
something gets shot up some
exponential power, basically.

00:49:32.710 --> 00:49:34.923
And, yeah, so the big
open question here,

00:49:34.923 --> 00:49:37.090
a big open direction that
I really want to highlight

00:49:37.090 --> 00:49:38.955
is that these
techniques are great,

00:49:38.955 --> 00:49:40.330
and they've gotten
us really far.

00:49:40.330 --> 00:49:42.670
But I think we
need something new.

00:49:42.670 --> 00:49:45.100
So either a new way to
combine these techniques,

00:49:45.100 --> 00:49:48.250
or different techniques in
order to make these algorithms

00:49:48.250 --> 00:49:49.840
actually practical, right?

00:49:49.840 --> 00:49:51.700
Those punch lines that I said
on the previous slide sound

00:49:51.700 --> 00:49:53.200
like they should
be super practical,

00:49:53.200 --> 00:49:55.540
but no one is going to use
this result in practice

00:49:55.540 --> 00:49:56.500
for very good reasons.

00:49:56.500 --> 00:50:02.070
And, yeah, I think that
new techniques are needed.

00:50:02.070 --> 00:50:02.570
Yeah.

00:50:02.570 --> 00:50:05.960
So this is something I
really want to highlight.

00:50:05.960 --> 00:50:06.710
Great.

00:50:06.710 --> 00:50:07.210
OK.

00:50:07.210 --> 00:50:08.168
So I have five minutes?

00:50:08.168 --> 00:50:08.752
STUDENT: Sure.

00:50:08.752 --> 00:50:09.530
PROFESSOR: Cool.

00:50:09.530 --> 00:50:10.030
OK.

00:50:10.030 --> 00:50:12.940
So maybe I do even have
time for my Technique 4.

00:50:12.940 --> 00:50:13.440
Yeah.

00:50:13.440 --> 00:50:16.490
So those are Techniques 1,
2, and 3 can be very useful,

00:50:16.490 --> 00:50:18.590
but I think there's
a lot of work

00:50:18.590 --> 00:50:21.320
to be done to make these
applicable in actually

00:50:21.320 --> 00:50:22.730
practical settings.

00:50:22.730 --> 00:50:26.390
I want to talk a little
bit about Technique 4,

00:50:26.390 --> 00:50:29.760
which I think is maybe more
in the direction of that.

00:50:29.760 --> 00:50:31.760
And this is exploiting
structure that's

00:50:31.760 --> 00:50:35.000
maybe already present in
the codes or the algorithms

00:50:35.000 --> 00:50:36.470
that we have so
that we don't have

00:50:36.470 --> 00:50:40.430
to apply these techniques
on top necessarily.

00:50:40.430 --> 00:50:41.780
And I have five minutes.

00:50:41.780 --> 00:50:43.290
So what's the best
way to do this?

00:50:43.290 --> 00:50:43.790
Yeah.

00:50:43.790 --> 00:50:45.500
So maybe I'll just
give the intuition.

00:50:45.500 --> 00:50:48.358
So, like, let's
recall from Talk 1--

00:50:48.358 --> 00:50:50.150
so this is the bit that
I rushed at the end

00:50:50.150 --> 00:50:51.200
because I really
wanted to highlight it,

00:50:51.200 --> 00:50:54.110
so it's good that I'm getting
to this bit because I did that.

00:50:54.110 --> 00:50:56.210
The Guruswamy-Lang decoder
that we talked about,

00:50:56.210 --> 00:50:57.627
the only thing I
told you about it

00:50:57.627 --> 00:50:58.877
is that it outputs a subspace.

00:50:58.877 --> 00:51:00.919
It outputs a constant
dimensional subspace that's

00:51:00.919 --> 00:51:02.220
guaranteed to contain a list.

00:51:02.220 --> 00:51:05.700
And it turns out other decoders,
as well, also output similarly

00:51:05.700 --> 00:51:06.782
structured lists.

00:51:06.782 --> 00:51:08.490
So things that live
in this interpolating

00:51:08.490 --> 00:51:10.260
polynomial and find
the root paradigm,

00:51:10.260 --> 00:51:14.370
a lot of times they output
some nice structures.

00:51:14.370 --> 00:51:17.880
And so let's let curly L be the
list that we want to return.

00:51:17.880 --> 00:51:19.320
So it's all of the
code words that

00:51:19.320 --> 00:51:21.300
are close to our
received word here.

00:51:21.300 --> 00:51:23.490
So if we know that our thing--

00:51:23.490 --> 00:51:26.130
that our algorithm
outputs a subspace,

00:51:26.130 --> 00:51:27.738
well, that tells
us that curly L is

00:51:27.738 --> 00:51:29.530
contained in some low
dimensional subspace.

00:51:29.530 --> 00:51:30.930
So maybe the picture
looks more like this.

00:51:30.930 --> 00:51:32.370
Like here are all my code words.

00:51:32.370 --> 00:51:35.130
Here's this low
dimensional subspace that's

00:51:35.130 --> 00:51:37.410
guaranteed to contain the list.

00:51:37.410 --> 00:51:41.580
But what if my code avoids
such structure, right?

00:51:41.580 --> 00:51:44.250
Then I know that actually
the list is smaller

00:51:44.250 --> 00:51:45.782
than I thought it was.

00:51:45.782 --> 00:51:47.490
It's not going to be
this whole subspace,

00:51:47.490 --> 00:51:50.247
it's just where the subspace
intersects the code, right?

00:51:50.247 --> 00:51:52.830
So that means that the list is
smaller than we thought it was.

00:51:52.830 --> 00:51:56.370
And this leads us to a
few different avenues

00:51:56.370 --> 00:51:57.570
to try to exploit this.

00:51:57.570 --> 00:52:00.990
So the first is that we
can try to modify the code,

00:52:00.990 --> 00:52:04.020
maybe intersect it with
some subspace basis sent,

00:52:04.020 --> 00:52:05.490
something that avoids subspaces.

00:52:05.490 --> 00:52:10.110
Or take advantage of some
other more precise structure

00:52:10.110 --> 00:52:11.572
that the decoder gives us.

00:52:11.572 --> 00:52:13.530
And there are lots of
works that have done that

00:52:13.530 --> 00:52:15.630
to very good effect.

00:52:15.630 --> 00:52:19.267
And then there's another
version which is--

00:52:19.267 --> 00:52:20.850
actually, we can
assert that the codes

00:52:20.850 --> 00:52:22.410
that we are interested
in themselves

00:52:22.410 --> 00:52:23.640
already had that structure.

00:52:23.640 --> 00:52:26.070
We don't even need to
do anything to them.

00:52:26.070 --> 00:52:28.170
And I find this very
promising because I

00:52:28.170 --> 00:52:30.540
said every time we do one
of these cool techniques,

00:52:30.540 --> 00:52:31.500
things get--

00:52:31.500 --> 00:52:33.360
something else gets
swept under the rug.

00:52:33.360 --> 00:52:34.980
And so with this
type of approach,

00:52:34.980 --> 00:52:36.720
we don't need to
do that for today.

00:52:36.720 --> 00:52:39.870
So here's an example
of how that would work.

00:52:39.870 --> 00:52:43.260
Suppose that C is a linear
code with good distance.

00:52:43.260 --> 00:52:47.250
And suppose that curly L is this
list that we want to return.

00:52:47.250 --> 00:52:49.890
And suppose that we know that
the list is contained in some,

00:52:49.890 --> 00:52:52.430
let's say, constant
dimensional subspace.

00:52:52.430 --> 00:52:55.670
Then I claim that automatically,
just from the distance of C,

00:52:55.670 --> 00:52:57.440
curly L is going to be smaller--

00:52:57.440 --> 00:52:59.732
it's going to be much smaller
than that low dimensional

00:52:59.732 --> 00:53:01.320
subspace.

00:53:01.320 --> 00:53:02.977
Even if curly C
is a linear code,

00:53:02.977 --> 00:53:04.560
it definitely doesn't
avoid subspaces.

00:53:04.560 --> 00:53:05.670
It is a subspace.

00:53:05.670 --> 00:53:07.410
But it will still--

00:53:07.410 --> 00:53:08.910
the list will still
be much smaller

00:53:08.910 --> 00:53:11.320
than all of the subspace.

00:53:11.320 --> 00:53:12.490
So why is this true?

00:53:12.490 --> 00:53:14.160
So here's the basic
idea, and this

00:53:14.160 --> 00:53:17.640
is from a joint work with
Kopparty, Ron-Zewi and Saraf

00:53:17.640 --> 00:53:19.720
from a few years back.

00:53:19.720 --> 00:53:23.940
So suppose that here is
this received code word

00:53:23.940 --> 00:53:26.102
G. I want to find
all of the code words

00:53:26.102 --> 00:53:28.560
that are close to-- or sorry,
not code word-- received word

00:53:28.560 --> 00:53:31.675
G. I want to find all the code
words that are close to G.

00:53:31.675 --> 00:53:33.050
So what I'm going
to do first is,

00:53:33.050 --> 00:53:35.840
let's choose a constant
number of random coordinates

00:53:35.840 --> 00:53:37.520
and just assume that
they're correct.

00:53:37.520 --> 00:53:39.650
So I'll pick a bunch
of random coordinates

00:53:39.650 --> 00:53:41.120
and be like those are right.

00:53:41.120 --> 00:53:43.820
I say so, right?

00:53:43.820 --> 00:53:47.540
And I claim that when I
do that that for any C,

00:53:47.540 --> 00:53:51.380
for any code word C in the list,
there is a decent probability

00:53:51.380 --> 00:53:53.960
you might be small, but
bounded away from zero,

00:53:53.960 --> 00:53:56.780
that C is the only
thing in my subspace W

00:53:56.780 --> 00:53:59.340
that agrees with this advice.

00:53:59.340 --> 00:54:00.670
So why is that the case?

00:54:00.670 --> 00:54:02.550
So that this has two components.

00:54:02.550 --> 00:54:06.000
First, that C does
agree with this advice.

00:54:06.000 --> 00:54:06.820
Why is that true?

00:54:06.820 --> 00:54:08.220
So C is likely to
agree with the advice

00:54:08.220 --> 00:54:09.420
just because it's in the list.

00:54:09.420 --> 00:54:11.128
That means it's close
to G. So it doesn't

00:54:11.128 --> 00:54:12.722
differ on too many places.

00:54:12.722 --> 00:54:14.430
So if I pick a constant
number of places,

00:54:14.430 --> 00:54:15.690
there's some decent
probability I won't

00:54:15.690 --> 00:54:17.040
hit any of the differences.

00:54:17.040 --> 00:54:20.940
So C is likely to agree
in these positions with G.

00:54:20.940 --> 00:54:22.680
And then I also need
to show that nothing

00:54:22.680 --> 00:54:26.400
else in the subspace
is likely to agree.

00:54:26.400 --> 00:54:29.370
And intuitively, the
reason this is true

00:54:29.370 --> 00:54:33.420
is, OK, so I have some very
low dimensional subspace,

00:54:33.420 --> 00:54:35.593
some constant
dimensional subspace,

00:54:35.593 --> 00:54:37.260
and it's a constant
dimensional subspace

00:54:37.260 --> 00:54:38.940
of a code with good distance.

00:54:38.940 --> 00:54:42.510
So that means that all of the
code words in that subspace

00:54:42.510 --> 00:54:44.310
are really far from each other.

00:54:44.310 --> 00:54:49.470
And you can use that to show
that, basically, every time you

00:54:49.470 --> 00:54:53.250
grab a random position
here and fix it,

00:54:53.250 --> 00:54:55.980
you're basically cutting down
the dimension of your solution

00:54:55.980 --> 00:54:57.240
space by 1.

00:54:57.240 --> 00:54:58.740
You can check with
high probability,

00:54:58.740 --> 00:55:00.450
this is going to knock down
the dimension of your solution

00:55:00.450 --> 00:55:02.153
space because the distance.

00:55:02.153 --> 00:55:03.570
And so what that
means is that you

00:55:03.570 --> 00:55:05.250
have a constant dimensional
space to start with.

00:55:05.250 --> 00:55:07.470
If you knock it down a
constant number of times,

00:55:07.470 --> 00:55:09.262
you won't have any
degrees of freedom left.

00:55:09.262 --> 00:55:13.180
So c will be the unique
thing in that list.

00:55:13.180 --> 00:55:13.780
OK, great.

00:55:13.780 --> 00:55:15.763
So if you believe
that, then, all right,

00:55:15.763 --> 00:55:16.930
what's the size of the list?

00:55:16.930 --> 00:55:18.970
Well, for anything
in the list, if I

00:55:18.970 --> 00:55:21.640
have this same algorithm that
returns it with probability P,

00:55:21.640 --> 00:55:24.055
so if you just do linearity
of expectation and stare

00:55:24.055 --> 00:55:25.930
at it for a bit, you'll
see that that implies

00:55:25.930 --> 00:55:27.722
that the size of the
list is no more than 1

00:55:27.722 --> 00:55:30.160
over P, which it actually
turns out with these parameters

00:55:30.160 --> 00:55:32.410
it's going to be much, much
smaller than the size of W

00:55:32.410 --> 00:55:33.740
itself.

00:55:33.740 --> 00:55:37.537
So you can use this for any
code with good distance.

00:55:37.537 --> 00:55:39.370
If the code has a little
bit more structure,

00:55:39.370 --> 00:55:41.373
you can actually do
something even better.

00:55:41.373 --> 00:55:42.790
So what you get
out is that folded

00:55:42.790 --> 00:55:46.112
Reed-Solomon codes
themselves, also univariate

00:55:46.112 --> 00:55:48.070
multiplicity codes which
I haven't talked about

00:55:48.070 --> 00:55:51.550
but which we'll talk about in
his talk tomorrow, I think.

00:55:51.550 --> 00:55:53.050
Folded Reed-Solomon
codes themselves

00:55:53.050 --> 00:55:55.330
with none of these fancy
techniques themselves

00:55:55.330 --> 00:55:59.080
are already list decodable
with rate R radius optimal,

00:55:59.080 --> 00:56:03.910
and list size smaller
than it was before.

00:56:03.910 --> 00:56:08.800
Although, the algorithms
are still no faster.

00:56:08.800 --> 00:56:11.380
Follow up work by Kopparty,
Resch, Ron-Zewi, Saraf

00:56:11.380 --> 00:56:14.090
and Silas derandomized
this decoder, the decoder

00:56:14.090 --> 00:56:15.590
I described as
randomized algorithm,

00:56:15.590 --> 00:56:16.940
take a bunch of random advice.

00:56:16.940 --> 00:56:18.440
They showed how to
derandomize this.

00:56:18.440 --> 00:56:20.420
And actually, a very
recent work by Tamo,

00:56:20.420 --> 00:56:23.420
this is like barely a month
old, has a simplified proof

00:56:23.420 --> 00:56:24.350
and better bounds.

00:56:24.350 --> 00:56:25.320
This is really pretty--

00:56:25.320 --> 00:56:26.653
I'm still processing this paper.

00:56:29.360 --> 00:56:31.730
But I think it is still open,
what is the correct list

00:56:31.730 --> 00:56:32.840
size for both of these.

00:56:32.840 --> 00:56:35.380
So if you're looking
for a problem.

00:56:35.380 --> 00:56:35.880
Cool.

00:56:35.880 --> 00:56:36.380
OK.

00:56:36.380 --> 00:56:38.003
So I should probably stop.

00:56:38.003 --> 00:56:38.670
Yeah, all right.

00:56:38.670 --> 00:56:41.290
So let me wrap up real quick.

00:56:41.290 --> 00:56:41.790
Cool.

00:56:41.790 --> 00:56:42.510
So what have we seen?

00:56:42.510 --> 00:56:44.302
We've seen a bunch of
techniques to improve

00:56:44.302 --> 00:56:46.530
this basic paradigm of
interpolated polynomial

00:56:46.530 --> 00:56:47.460
and find some roots.

00:56:47.460 --> 00:56:49.290
So Technique 1 is locality.

00:56:49.290 --> 00:56:50.910
Technique 2 is
distance amplification.

00:56:50.910 --> 00:56:53.430
Technique 3 is reduce
the alphabet size.

00:56:53.430 --> 00:56:56.790
And we saw that you can
apply all of these things

00:56:56.790 --> 00:56:58.812
all together to get very
good results on paper,

00:56:58.812 --> 00:57:00.270
but there's a lot
of open questions

00:57:00.270 --> 00:57:02.550
when it comes to making
these actually practical.

00:57:02.550 --> 00:57:07.410
And then Technique 4 is, we
can also additionally exploit

00:57:07.410 --> 00:57:10.020
some structure in the
codes or the algorithm.

00:57:10.020 --> 00:57:12.840
And yeah-- and I
guess I did this

00:57:12.840 --> 00:57:15.930
after because I didn't actually
expect to be able to get to it.

00:57:15.930 --> 00:57:17.088
But you can lump this in.

00:57:17.088 --> 00:57:18.630
You can do all four
of them together.

00:57:18.630 --> 00:57:21.180
You can still get lots of--

00:57:21.180 --> 00:57:22.240
all of the things.

00:57:22.240 --> 00:57:22.740
Right.

00:57:22.740 --> 00:57:24.282
And, of course,
yeah, as I mentioned,

00:57:24.282 --> 00:57:26.023
there's still lots left to do.

00:57:26.023 --> 00:57:27.690
So those are the
techniques that we saw.

00:57:27.690 --> 00:57:29.560
How about the open questions?

00:57:29.560 --> 00:57:32.040
So I mentioned a lot of open
questions for binary codes,

00:57:32.040 --> 00:57:32.540
right?

00:57:32.540 --> 00:57:34.380
So practical near
linear time decoders

00:57:34.380 --> 00:57:37.080
for probabilistic constructions
of binary codes near the GV

00:57:37.080 --> 00:57:37.660
bound.

00:57:37.660 --> 00:57:39.240
And then, of course,
explicit constructions

00:57:39.240 --> 00:57:40.615
of binary codes
near the GV bound

00:57:40.615 --> 00:57:43.440
is the question in
more parameter regimes

00:57:43.440 --> 00:57:45.000
than we currently have them.

00:57:45.000 --> 00:57:47.790
And over large alphabets,
again, practical

00:57:47.790 --> 00:57:50.520
near linear time, list decoding,
or list recovery algorithms

00:57:50.520 --> 00:57:51.630
and various sizes.

00:57:51.630 --> 00:57:53.550
And I think if we
could get these,

00:57:53.550 --> 00:57:57.550
hopefully, I've
convinced you that if we

00:57:57.550 --> 00:58:00.778
can get really practical things,
even if the alphabet size is

00:58:00.778 --> 00:58:02.820
huge, then with this sort
of concatenation trait,

00:58:02.820 --> 00:58:07.080
we can, perhaps, turn that
into better unique decoding

00:58:07.080 --> 00:58:09.060
algorithms for binary codes.

00:58:09.060 --> 00:58:10.920
But both might require
new techniques.

00:58:10.920 --> 00:58:13.440
So maybe instead of my
little cartoon hammer,

00:58:13.440 --> 00:58:16.620
we need maybe a fancier
hammer, or my hope

00:58:16.620 --> 00:58:19.170
is that we just need a simpler
tool that we haven't thought

00:58:19.170 --> 00:58:21.180
of, like an Ikea Allen
wrench, I'm hoping,

00:58:21.180 --> 00:58:23.300
is something we
should be going for.

00:58:23.300 --> 00:58:24.085
But who knows?

00:58:24.085 --> 00:58:26.460
So hopefully, we'll figure it
out in the coming semester.

00:58:26.460 --> 00:58:27.360
OK.
So I'll stop there.

00:58:27.360 --> 00:58:28.193
It's time for lunch.

00:58:28.193 --> 00:58:33.486
[APPLAUSE]

00:58:33.486 --> 00:58:37.130
STUDENT: Thanks for having
tutorial and a good time

00:58:37.130 --> 00:58:39.130
for a quick question if
people have any, or have

00:58:39.130 --> 00:58:40.735
any questions during the talk.

00:58:40.735 --> 00:58:43.780
STUDENT: I just want to confirm
the reason he's answering is

00:58:43.780 --> 00:58:45.850
that the article doesn't have--

00:58:45.850 --> 00:58:50.360
AG code doesn't have the linear
time sensor to reduce from--

00:58:50.360 --> 00:58:51.610
PROFESSOR: That's right, yeah.

00:58:51.610 --> 00:58:55.900
So the question is like, why
do we need to tensor things up?

00:58:55.900 --> 00:58:57.563
Yes, because the
original AG code,

00:58:57.563 --> 00:58:59.230
if we want a near
linear time algorithm,

00:58:59.230 --> 00:59:00.610
it doesn't have nearly
time algorithms.

00:59:00.610 --> 00:59:03.178
But after you tensor it up,
then it becomes [INAUDIBLE]..

00:59:06.022 --> 00:59:08.662
STUDENT: Question?

00:59:08.662 --> 00:59:11.225
STUDENT: Is there ever a reason
to tensor different codes

00:59:11.225 --> 00:59:11.725
together?

00:59:11.725 --> 00:59:13.330
PROFESSOR: Yes.

00:59:13.330 --> 00:59:14.755
STUDENT: What would be one?

00:59:14.755 --> 00:59:15.380
PROFESSOR: Yes.

00:59:15.380 --> 00:59:17.130
So the question is,
is there ever a reason

00:59:17.130 --> 00:59:18.880
to tensor different
codes together.

00:59:22.180 --> 00:59:22.720
Yeah.

00:59:22.720 --> 00:59:23.890
So like for some--

00:59:23.890 --> 00:59:25.990
actually, I think in that
very paper we actually--

00:59:25.990 --> 00:59:27.010
I think I was oversimplifying.

00:59:27.010 --> 00:59:28.660
I think we do want
different codes.

00:59:28.660 --> 00:59:32.320
But like one for
algorithms, sometimes you

00:59:32.320 --> 00:59:35.380
want one of the codes to have
some nice algorithmic property,

00:59:35.380 --> 00:59:37.630
and the other one to have a
different nice algorithmic

00:59:37.630 --> 00:59:39.910
property because then sometimes
the decoding algorithm goes

00:59:39.910 --> 00:59:41.260
back and forth and leverages.

00:59:41.260 --> 00:59:43.330
So, yeah, there are
reasons that you'd want

00:59:43.330 --> 00:59:44.950
to tensor two different things.

00:59:44.950 --> 00:59:46.768
I just didn't talk about it.

00:59:46.768 --> 00:59:47.560
STUDENT: All right.

